WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.940 --> 00:00:09.800
Welcome to Science in the Hands of Scientists.

00:00:09.800 --> 00:00:18.300
I'm Carl Zimmer and this afternoon we're going
to have a conversation about how science happens.

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:22.650
You know we have to bear in mind science is
not something that happens in the abstract.

00:00:22.650 --> 00:00:24.190
People do science.

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:31.730
And so these are not you know gods who are
impervious to the forces that shape us.

00:00:31.730 --> 00:00:37.950
They're human like the rest of us and they
deal with all sorts of factors that everybody

00:00:37.950 --> 00:00:39.080
else does.

00:00:39.080 --> 00:00:47.170
It can be everything from pressures to get
a job, to get funding.

00:00:47.170 --> 00:00:51.269
It can be internal biases, there can be the
thirst for glory.

00:00:51.269 --> 00:00:56.329
There are all sorts of things that can shape
how scientists decide to do science and what

00:00:56.329 --> 00:00:58.090
they choose to do.

00:00:58.090 --> 00:01:01.039
And that's what we're going to be talking
about this this afternoon.

00:01:01.039 --> 00:01:06.630
We're going to have an open discussion about
the scientific process and how it works from

00:01:06.630 --> 00:01:12.840
the classroom to the lab to the journal and
then out into the rest of the world.

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:17.040
So let me start now to introduce our panelists.

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:19.570
Our first guest this afternoon.

00:01:19.570 --> 00:01:24.610
She is a principal investigator at the Tufts
Center For Regenerative and Developmental

00:01:24.610 --> 00:01:25.610
Biology.

00:01:25.610 --> 00:01:29.630
Please welcome Dany Spencer Adams.

00:01:29.630 --> 00:01:34.260
Our next guest is the K.D.

00:01:34.260 --> 00:01:38.370
Irani Professor of Philosophy at City College
of New York.

00:01:38.370 --> 00:01:42.830
He works on the philosophy of science and
the nature of pseudoscience.

00:01:42.830 --> 00:01:49.910
Please welcome Massimo Pigliucci.

00:01:49.910 --> 00:01:55.680
Our third guest today is a distinguished writer
in residence at New York University's Arthur

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:58.140
Carter Journalism Institute.

00:01:58.140 --> 00:02:01.630
He's the co-founder of Retraction Watch.

00:02:01.630 --> 00:02:06.470
Full disclosure I have written about his work
for the New York Times.

00:02:06.470 --> 00:02:11.670
So please welcome Ivan Oransky.

00:02:11.670 --> 00:02:16.950
And last but certainly not least our final
guest is the editor in chief of Scientific

00:02:16.950 --> 00:02:17.950
American.

00:02:17.950 --> 00:02:22.500
She also oversees the journalists both the
Scientific American and at the Nature Journals..

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:27.270
Please welcome Mariette DiChristina.

00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:30.060
So now that I have you all out.

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:36.170
Let's just start by just kinda talking about
how you make a scientist?

00:02:36.170 --> 00:02:38.080
And maybe Dany I could start with you.

00:02:38.080 --> 00:02:45.950
I mean so you have a lab and you are bringing
in students at Tufts who want to be scientists.

00:02:45.950 --> 00:02:51.140
So how do you take them from being students
to people who are going to do science in the

00:02:51.140 --> 00:02:52.140
21st century?

00:02:52.140 --> 00:03:01.090
I would describe it mostly as like an internship
or or you.

00:03:01.090 --> 00:03:04.000
Most of it is just being thrown right in.

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:13.510
Here, do this and there's a lot of supervising,
teaching specific things, specific skills,

00:03:13.510 --> 00:03:16.040
specific techniques, protocols.

00:03:16.040 --> 00:03:22.670
And then there's the other it's more of a
meta kind of approach where you have to teach

00:03:22.670 --> 00:03:28.600
really that, you can call scientific thinking,
you could call it scientific method, but really

00:03:28.600 --> 00:03:33.090
the idea that science is simply one way of
learning things.

00:03:33.090 --> 00:03:40.709
And it's a it's been a very very powerful
very good way of learning things and it's

00:03:40.709 --> 00:03:43.989
in some ways not not human-like.

00:03:43.989 --> 00:03:46.730
I don't want to say inhuman.

00:03:46.730 --> 00:03:51.960
I don't want to say super human but humans,
and I'm very much looking forward to the rest

00:03:51.960 --> 00:03:57.380
of my panel to talk about this, but humans
have what's called confirmation bias and we

00:03:57.380 --> 00:04:03.870
tend to recognize and remember the things
that confirm what we already believe.

00:04:03.870 --> 00:04:07.780
And I just throw this out, most people if
you think about the newspapers you read.

00:04:07.780 --> 00:04:11.849
You're not, most of us me included, I'm not
looking for new information.

00:04:11.849 --> 00:04:15.540
I'm looking for confirmation for what I already
believe.

00:04:15.540 --> 00:04:16.590
And you can say I knew it.

00:04:16.590 --> 00:04:17.590
I knew it.

00:04:17.590 --> 00:04:22.019
Yeah I knew that he shouldn't have been elected.

00:04:22.019 --> 00:04:23.800
No names.

00:04:23.800 --> 00:04:31.560
And I think that one really useful way to
think about science as a way of learning is

00:04:31.560 --> 00:04:36.750
it's about overcoming confirmation bias.

00:04:36.750 --> 00:04:46.520
So it's about phrasing a question in a way
that allows you to ignore what you believe

00:04:46.520 --> 00:04:49.810
or even say OK, here's my idea.

00:04:49.810 --> 00:04:51.770
Now I'm going to try and prove myself wrong.

00:04:51.770 --> 00:04:57.630
And I can't think of any other way of learning
or thinking where that's what you do where

00:04:57.630 --> 00:05:03.790
you say here's my terrific idea and now I'm
going to try and kill it.

00:05:03.790 --> 00:05:04.790
And.

00:05:04.790 --> 00:05:05.790
So.

00:05:05.790 --> 00:05:11.380
Learning the intellectual skills of thinking
that way and asking questions that way and

00:05:11.380 --> 00:05:16.120
controls are all about proving that what you
thought was going to happen wouldn't happen

00:05:16.120 --> 00:05:20.080
that it was actually the injection you did
or it was actually because the air conditioner

00:05:20.080 --> 00:05:22.690
was on or it was Thursday.

00:05:22.690 --> 00:05:29.230
And I think that that that is what makes it
so powerful is that it's it's about overcoming

00:05:29.230 --> 00:05:33.830
confirmation bias so that you can see what's
there rather than what you were expecting.

00:05:33.830 --> 00:05:42.110
Do you find that you have to kind of push
them further with when they're sort of satisfied

00:05:42.110 --> 00:05:47.200
that they have done enough to try to prove
themselves wrong, looks like I'm right.

00:05:47.200 --> 00:05:52.000
Yeah absolutely it's it's.

00:05:52.000 --> 00:05:56.840
There's there's you know it's crushing, it's
ego crushing.

00:05:56.840 --> 00:06:00.180
You come in and you're so excited that you're
going to get to have wonderful conversations

00:06:00.180 --> 00:06:04.870
and and there's fantastic questions and you're
going to find the answers and you're going

00:06:04.870 --> 00:06:05.990
to change the world.

00:06:05.990 --> 00:06:06.990
And.

00:06:06.990 --> 00:06:09.560
First of all it's not like that.

00:06:09.560 --> 00:06:14.840
It takes a very long time but you have to
constantly say alright how would you disprove

00:06:14.840 --> 00:06:16.030
that?

00:06:16.030 --> 00:06:17.290
How would you disprove that?

00:06:17.290 --> 00:06:18.540
What's the proper control?

00:06:18.540 --> 00:06:24.169
How do you know that, you know, how are you
going to interpret it if you get one of these

00:06:24.169 --> 00:06:25.530
three possible results?

00:06:25.530 --> 00:06:29.130
And what are you going to do if you get something
that you didn't predict at all?

00:06:29.130 --> 00:06:33.521
It's there's it's you have to teach that and
the only way to teach that that we know of

00:06:33.521 --> 00:06:36.190
at this point is simply to do it.

00:06:36.190 --> 00:06:38.960
So students come in.

00:06:38.960 --> 00:06:40.759
And.

00:06:40.759 --> 00:06:44.699
You know when you get them started and then
you say OK no, that's wrong and that's wrong

00:06:44.699 --> 00:06:46.400
and that's wrong and that's wrong.

00:06:46.400 --> 00:06:50.280
And there's a there's a saying if you're not
failing most of the time you're not working

00:06:50.280 --> 00:06:54.840
hard enough and that's a that's a hard thing
to get students through.

00:06:54.840 --> 00:06:56.970
Absolutely yeah.

00:06:56.970 --> 00:07:02.949
And Massimo, you have a long career not only
as a philosopher but but as a scientist and

00:07:02.949 --> 00:07:07.230
have you know trained your own...

00:07:07.230 --> 00:07:08.620
Yeah.

00:07:08.620 --> 00:07:10.530
So I was in evolutionary biology.

00:07:10.530 --> 00:07:14.199
I tend to agree with most of what you just
said.

00:07:14.199 --> 00:07:18.880
I would even go even further in terms of I'm
a little more pessimistic about how the whole

00:07:18.880 --> 00:07:19.880
thing works.

00:07:19.880 --> 00:07:27.120
So ideally actually I think that lab works
a lot like a renaissance place where you go

00:07:27.120 --> 00:07:30.389
and learn from Michelangelo from you know
Leonardo or whatever it is.

00:07:30.389 --> 00:07:31.389
Yeah

00:07:31.389 --> 00:07:35.800
It's a workshop you come in and you learn
because you are in constant contact and interaction

00:07:35.800 --> 00:07:40.070
with not only the master, that would be the
PI, the principal investigator of the lab

00:07:40.070 --> 00:07:44.139
but also the people that have been there for
a long time so senior students or post-docs

00:07:44.139 --> 00:07:45.289
or things like that, right?

00:07:45.289 --> 00:07:51.150
So you are immersed in an intellectual environment
and you're doing things every day and that's

00:07:51.150 --> 00:07:53.080
how you learn how to do science.

00:07:53.080 --> 00:07:58.120
But I honestly don't think actually that individually
we can as much as we it's a good idea to be

00:07:58.120 --> 00:08:02.250
aware of it and try I don't think we can individually
overcome our confirmation bias.

00:08:02.250 --> 00:08:07.080
The way it happens most of the time is precisely
because the rest of the people in the lab,

00:08:07.080 --> 00:08:10.620
particularly the principal investigator tell
you no, that's not going to work.

00:08:10.620 --> 00:08:12.800
Why don't you think about it that way.

00:08:12.800 --> 00:08:17.389
And of course who does that for the PI, who
watch who watches the watchman?

00:08:17.389 --> 00:08:22.000
That's the peer review outside of the lab
right so you have the PI himself or herself

00:08:22.000 --> 00:08:27.250
has to get a grant proposal approved, those
have to go through the process of peer review.

00:08:27.250 --> 00:08:31.569
Even if they are approved and the research
gets done, then you have to publish the results.

00:08:31.569 --> 00:08:35.860
And yes it is ego crushing, I mean it's rejection
over and over and over.

00:08:35.860 --> 00:08:39.480
And you just have to grow a thick skin and
deal with it.

00:08:39.480 --> 00:08:42.230
But that's the way you learn it by doing it.

00:08:42.230 --> 00:08:43.230
So, so.

00:08:43.230 --> 00:08:49.750
When you when you look at the you know the
cohort of new scientists you know coming out

00:08:49.750 --> 00:08:57.060
you know freshly minted Ph.D. in 2017, you
know what's it like for them starting out

00:08:57.060 --> 00:09:00.510
now in science as opposed to 1997, 1987?

00:09:00.510 --> 00:09:02.910
Is it the same?

00:09:02.910 --> 00:09:03.920
Is it different?

00:09:03.920 --> 00:09:08.070
In some respects it's the same it's the excitement
is still there.

00:09:08.070 --> 00:09:13.880
And it's a great time for science for biology
for physics for for other disciplines but

00:09:13.880 --> 00:09:17.450
at the same time you're also very conscious
these days that you know making a career in

00:09:17.450 --> 00:09:19.399
academia, it's not it's not easy.

00:09:19.399 --> 00:09:24.270
The number of people, typically when we advertise
for one position there's like 150 applicants

00:09:24.270 --> 00:09:25.270
easily.

00:09:25.270 --> 00:09:28.950
And I guarantee you a lot of them are actually
qualified for that position.

00:09:28.950 --> 00:09:35.060
So it gets you know that you get into a period
of possibly several years where you go from

00:09:35.060 --> 00:09:38.100
one post doc to the other or from one adjunct
position to the other.

00:09:38.100 --> 00:09:42.230
And if you're lucky you're actually going
to make it by the time you're in the mid-thirties

00:09:42.230 --> 00:09:45.900
or something into a beginning of a tenure,
tenure track.

00:09:45.900 --> 00:09:47.100
So things are changing in that sense.

00:09:47.100 --> 00:09:53.340
The job market in the sciences has gotten
worse over decades.

00:09:53.340 --> 00:09:58.640
I mean the real peak period that was in the
1960s and early 70s where you could just go

00:09:58.640 --> 00:10:00.290
out and get a job.

00:10:00.290 --> 00:10:04.870
It hasn't been like that for a while and it's
increasingly less so.

00:10:04.870 --> 00:10:06.050
But we also have a lot of talent.

00:10:06.050 --> 00:10:10.360
We have a lot of people that I have you know
you do it because you have you're excited

00:10:10.360 --> 00:10:11.360
about it.

00:10:11.360 --> 00:10:14.550
You really have to want to experience the
joy of being a scientist in the lab.

00:10:14.550 --> 00:10:19.820
You don't do it for the money because it doesn't
really pay that well and you don't do it for

00:10:19.820 --> 00:10:22.910
the benefits because those come very late
in your career.

00:10:22.910 --> 00:10:24.400
You have to sacrifice a number of things.

00:10:24.400 --> 00:10:26.029
You do it because you love it.

00:10:26.029 --> 00:10:30.950
So Ivan and Mariette, I mean you're you're
kind of more on the outside looking in on

00:10:30.950 --> 00:10:38.240
this culture of science and I'm curious if
you see differences now in what it's like

00:10:38.240 --> 00:10:43.970
for scientists to be starting out and launching
their careers in science now as opposed to

00:10:43.970 --> 00:10:45.270
like when you started being journalists.

00:10:45.270 --> 00:10:48.850
I don't know which one of you wants to jump
in?

00:10:48.850 --> 00:10:54.670
I can start and then Mariette will probably
say something smarter and more scientific

00:10:54.670 --> 00:10:57.050
than I will, how's that?

00:10:57.050 --> 00:11:00.980
You know I was just struck, if I may for 10
seconds just say what Dany was saying about

00:11:00.980 --> 00:11:03.920
I think about confirmation bias a lot.

00:11:03.920 --> 00:11:08.200
Everything that you said about scientists
and how you really ideally want to wake up

00:11:08.200 --> 00:11:11.060
every morning going how can I prove myself
wrong?

00:11:11.060 --> 00:11:13.840
Well journalists are supposed to do that too.

00:11:13.840 --> 00:11:18.470
And you know I am not going to claim that
I do that I wake up usually and you know check

00:11:18.470 --> 00:11:19.470
Facebook.

00:11:19.470 --> 00:11:23.420
But you know you you want to wake up and you
need an editor you need someone who's saying

00:11:23.420 --> 00:11:24.420
well wait a second.

00:11:24.420 --> 00:11:28.370
You know yeah you have that document but that
document doesn't really say what you think.

00:11:28.370 --> 00:11:30.330
So I just want to reflect on that for a second.

00:11:30.330 --> 00:11:34.279
IVAN I think that there are two I wouldn't
call them changes necessarily but they’ve

00:11:34.279 --> 00:11:35.470
sort of accelerated.

00:11:35.470 --> 00:11:39.769
And then I'd be interested to hear what others
think about this but there were sort of sort

00:11:39.769 --> 00:11:42.600
of two phenomena that I think have accelerated.

00:11:42.600 --> 00:11:46.550
One is what I would refer to as a sort of
metricization.

00:11:46.550 --> 00:11:47.550
Right?

00:11:47.550 --> 00:11:49.030
So everything is now measured.

00:11:49.030 --> 00:11:52.920
And this is true in all walks of life, it’s
a sort of if you'll forgive me a little bit

00:11:52.920 --> 00:11:57.250
of not philosophy exactly, political science
of a neo liberal idea that you know we can

00:11:57.250 --> 00:11:58.610
measure everything.

00:11:58.610 --> 00:12:04.020
And so that includes measuring impact of research
and that includes something called the impact

00:12:04.020 --> 00:12:05.500
factor which is...

00:12:05.500 --> 00:12:09.089
Describe how you measure the impact of somebody's
study.

00:12:09.089 --> 00:12:10.089
How do you know?

00:12:10.089 --> 00:12:13.459
So, often you know what a lot of it is based
on and I'm not here to defend this but just

00:12:13.459 --> 00:12:18.080
to say that this is what happens is how often
are other scientists citing that work.

00:12:18.080 --> 00:12:22.459
So if a tree falls in the forest and no one
hears it did it really fall?

00:12:22.459 --> 00:12:26.830
If a paper is published and no one cites it
does it really matter?

00:12:26.830 --> 00:12:29.270
And again I'm not condoning that but that
is what matters.

00:12:29.270 --> 00:12:34.959
And so you turn that into a number, you turn
it into a number of numbers actually an ever-growing

00:12:34.959 --> 00:12:39.540
sort of forest of numbers where you actually
have something called impact factor.

00:12:39.540 --> 00:12:45.430
So the number of times on average I'm simplifying
here that a given, article in a given journal

00:12:45.430 --> 00:12:50.399
are published and a lot of people and I kind
of am in this camp a bit say that this has

00:12:50.399 --> 00:12:55.120
really had a dramatic effect on science and
not necessarily in a good way because everyone

00:12:55.120 --> 00:12:59.589
is pushed to publish in these high impact
factor journals.

00:12:59.589 --> 00:13:05.210
And so they publish a lot of really good science
but it also what it means is that tenure committees

00:13:05.210 --> 00:13:08.750
promotions anyone who sort of helps you to
get ahead or is what you need to get through

00:13:08.750 --> 00:13:12.649
to get a good peer review committees you know
Massimo was talking about peer reviews.

00:13:12.649 --> 00:13:14.320
They all are looking for these numbers.

00:13:14.320 --> 00:13:18.040
They're not necessarily reading the entire
paper anymore which is what you would hope

00:13:18.040 --> 00:13:19.040
they would do.

00:13:19.040 --> 00:13:22.220
But they're just looking at well is it in
this journal or that journal or what have

00:13:22.220 --> 00:13:23.220
you.

00:13:23.220 --> 00:13:26.910
The other thing very quickly that I think
is happening alongside this and really feeding

00:13:26.910 --> 00:13:31.980
into it as well is that there was a blip and
now it's sort of looking more and more like

00:13:31.980 --> 00:13:36.910
a blip, from 1998 to 2003 if you look at the
doubling.

00:13:36.910 --> 00:13:39.870
So the NIH budget and that’s not the only
budget…

00:13:39.870 --> 00:13:45.741
National Institute of Health, main funder
of biomedical research in the US.

00:13:45.741 --> 00:13:46.741
Exactly.

00:13:46.741 --> 00:13:49.630
The and this was a good thing for science
it meant that there were more there was more

00:13:49.630 --> 00:13:53.090
funding obviously, there was twice as much
funding over that period of time.

00:13:53.090 --> 00:13:54.090
But that stopped.

00:13:54.090 --> 00:13:57.352
And so in fact you could argue and if you
look at inflation the amount of funding, federal

00:13:57.352 --> 00:14:01.060
funding for the NIH has declined.

00:14:01.060 --> 00:14:06.240
And yet all of those graduate students, postdoc
trainees, well they were all working somewhere

00:14:06.240 --> 00:14:07.930
because labs just staffed up.

00:14:07.930 --> 00:14:11.160
It was like some kind of boom it really was
a boom.

00:14:11.160 --> 00:14:16.600
Well for this over the past whatever it is
14 years now we've gotten many many even more

00:14:16.600 --> 00:14:17.600
than we had.

00:14:17.600 --> 00:14:22.370
And it wasn't so good as as Massimo pointed
out since you know whether it's the 60s or

00:14:22.370 --> 00:14:23.370
early 70s.

00:14:23.370 --> 00:14:26.579
And so you had more and more people competing,
all needing to get all these numbers.

00:14:26.579 --> 00:14:32.650
And lo and behold we were seeing a lot more
competition and that, a little bit of competition

00:14:32.650 --> 00:14:33.880
is a really good thing.

00:14:33.880 --> 00:14:35.820
Too much competition can really harm you.

00:14:35.820 --> 00:14:36.820
Mariette?

00:14:36.820 --> 00:14:41.529
Thanks very much and by the way it's fascinating
just to listen so far.

00:14:41.529 --> 00:14:42.529
Absolutely wonderful.

00:14:42.529 --> 00:14:50.370
So I have two things I would like to add just
reflecting on the ideas of confirmation bias,

00:14:50.370 --> 00:14:53.029
on increasing competition, on impact factor.

00:14:53.029 --> 00:14:54.360
On the two areas.

00:14:54.360 --> 00:14:58.950
First of all I think a thing that we often
don't recognize is publishing's role in this

00:14:58.950 --> 00:15:03.200
ecosystem, in both at the popular level so
the Scientific American level where I'm editor

00:15:03.200 --> 00:15:04.450
in chief.

00:15:04.450 --> 00:15:12.890
One way we can either foster haste in publication
or not is how do we jump on "this week salt

00:15:12.890 --> 00:15:13.890
is bad for you.

00:15:13.890 --> 00:15:15.100
This week it's good for you" you know.

00:15:15.100 --> 00:15:22.200
Can we take the longer view provide context,
not be slaves to the embargo system, the system

00:15:22.200 --> 00:15:27.250
where you know we all agree that a paper is
going to come out a period of time from now

00:15:27.250 --> 00:15:32.460
and the journalists like me and like Ivan
we get a few days head notice to...and the

00:15:32.460 --> 00:15:37.089
idea is that we will we will we will all publish
our stories at the same time and we'll have

00:15:37.089 --> 00:15:43.410
had some some equal access to the experts
to do that without haste, but also comes with

00:15:43.410 --> 00:15:47.670
a whole host of other challenges which we
may, we may or may not come onto later.

00:15:47.670 --> 00:15:49.279
So that's that's one aspect.

00:15:49.279 --> 00:15:53.959
So mass publications like Scientific American
and then the journals themselves.

00:15:53.959 --> 00:15:59.160
How do we you know I am not a journal editor
although I work at a large publishing company

00:15:59.160 --> 00:16:04.370
Springer Nature which which publishes 2000
around journals.

00:16:04.370 --> 00:16:06.040
Including some of the biggest ones like Nature.

00:16:06.040 --> 00:16:09.820
Including some of the ones with very high
impact factor very selective journals like

00:16:09.820 --> 00:16:12.730
Nature and the Nature branded titles.

00:16:12.730 --> 00:16:18.220
And how do we set standards there that in
fact slow down that haste to publish that

00:16:18.220 --> 00:16:23.300
enable a little more checking and accuracy
like reproducibility check lists, like checking

00:16:23.300 --> 00:16:28.510
methods and protocols, like supporting the
research community so those pressures that

00:16:28.510 --> 00:16:34.839
people like Massimo are calling upon or noticing,
are are somewhat supported and slow down a

00:16:34.839 --> 00:16:35.839
little bit.

00:16:35.839 --> 00:16:39.500
And then I want to set the lens a tiny bit
more broadly outside of publishing.

00:16:39.500 --> 00:16:42.420
So how do we as a society look at science
and treat it?

00:16:42.420 --> 00:16:47.450
Do we jump for the latest headline or do we
start with our kids when they're very very

00:16:47.450 --> 00:16:48.450
small.

00:16:48.450 --> 00:16:53.070
Because researchers like Alison Gopnik will
point out we're all born researchers and you

00:16:53.070 --> 00:16:57.240
don't have to believe me just watch any kid
in a high chair dropping different things

00:16:57.240 --> 00:16:59.889
off to see how they fall and they bounce or
whatever.

00:16:59.889 --> 00:17:02.480
So we're all born experimenting.

00:17:02.480 --> 00:17:08.079
But over time in schools we're taught through
a series of classes that science is a set

00:17:08.079 --> 00:17:09.720
of received wisdom.

00:17:09.720 --> 00:17:15.049
And not something that you try and fail because
you know watching my own girls, one now in

00:17:15.049 --> 00:17:20.749
college, one in high school, every every experiment
they take in every science class has the right

00:17:20.749 --> 00:17:21.749
answer.

00:17:21.749 --> 00:17:23.619
So this is kind of how the mass society is
left.

00:17:23.619 --> 00:17:28.919
So I encourage us to look at those two lenses
you know one is the rule of publishing and

00:17:28.919 --> 00:17:32.230
the ecosystem in the broader role of society
at large.

00:17:32.230 --> 00:17:38.409
I have to say as a journalist when I was starting
out there there came a point, and it took

00:17:38.409 --> 00:17:47.289
a little while for me to actually appreciate
how much of the work of a scientist was filling

00:17:47.289 --> 00:17:50.730
out paperwork to get the money to do science.

00:17:50.730 --> 00:17:54.419
And like I just sort of thought like well
somehow like the money just sort of like shows

00:17:54.419 --> 00:17:58.440
up in a box or something and then the scientist
does the science, you know.

00:17:58.440 --> 00:18:02.840
I mean when you read a you know you read a
biography of Einstein you don't read him filling

00:18:02.840 --> 00:18:04.929
out an application to the National Science
Foundation.

00:18:04.929 --> 00:18:08.299
You know you want to know how he came up with
E = MC squared.

00:18:08.299 --> 00:18:13.220
But I just remember like I think that was
the one the one time it really hit me I went

00:18:13.220 --> 00:18:18.999
to have, to interview a scientist, a biologist,
like a leading evolutionary biologist.

00:18:18.999 --> 00:18:25.450
And I come into his office and he is like
hold on, I can't talk with you just yet.

00:18:25.450 --> 00:18:30.639
And he was on a conference call with these
co-authors who all wanted to get this grant

00:18:30.639 --> 00:18:34.970
and they were trying to figure out why it
was that their, their, their the essay they

00:18:34.970 --> 00:18:38.490
wrote to get this money didn't fit on one
page.

00:18:38.490 --> 00:18:41.659
Like there was something wrong with a font
and they couldn't figure out what was wrong

00:18:41.659 --> 00:18:42.659
with the font.

00:18:42.659 --> 00:18:46.570
Because they had like two or three words are
spilling off the end of this pdf and they're

00:18:46.570 --> 00:18:47.570
like ahhhh...

00:18:47.570 --> 00:18:50.940
If we don't get this all on one page there's
no chance we're going to get this money.

00:18:50.940 --> 00:18:55.019
There's no chance we're going to be making
this great insight...I'm just looking at them

00:18:55.019 --> 00:18:58.639
like I, I didn't come here for this.

00:18:58.639 --> 00:19:03.240
And I had to wait like 20 minutes for them
to like fix their friggin font and apply for

00:19:03.240 --> 00:19:09.359
the grant and then we could get to the science
and I'm just here wondering like.

00:19:09.359 --> 00:19:12.119
I mean that's the sort of stuff we tend not
to report on.

00:19:12.119 --> 00:19:14.299
But unless obviously like really....

00:19:14.299 --> 00:19:19.499
I mean that must be a big part of what goes
on in terms of like your time and then also

00:19:19.499 --> 00:19:24.250
how does that affect what you're going to
decide to study?

00:19:24.250 --> 00:19:29.529
Yeah it's a very frustrating part of it and
it's something that I do wish more people

00:19:29.529 --> 00:19:33.639
knew about because your tax dollars put me
through school.

00:19:33.639 --> 00:19:40.080
You trained me and I'm trained to be in the
lab and how to ask a question, how to design

00:19:40.080 --> 00:19:47.639
an experiment and I probably spend, I probably
spent 50 percent of my time writing the same

00:19:47.639 --> 00:19:52.729
thing over and over again, changing the font,
changing the format, making sure this thing

00:19:52.729 --> 00:19:54.159
is filled out and that's filled in.

00:19:54.159 --> 00:20:00.350
And you know we have a rule never ever update
your software within one week of the due date

00:20:00.350 --> 00:20:04.250
because it will get messed up.

00:20:04.250 --> 00:20:10.979
Don't send from a Mac to a PC because it'll
come back wrong and it's it's very frustrating

00:20:10.979 --> 00:20:11.979
to do that.

00:20:11.979 --> 00:20:19.190
And the bigger the labs so that the more productive
the lab should be and can be and is, the more

00:20:19.190 --> 00:20:24.039
time the PI's probably spent, sorry PI stands
for principal investigator and that's the

00:20:24.039 --> 00:20:27.359
big scientist in the lab.

00:20:27.359 --> 00:20:31.039
The person who gets all the credit even though
the work is done by many people.

00:20:31.039 --> 00:20:41.059
60 to 70 percent of the time is spent doing
the paperwork, writing the story.

00:20:41.059 --> 00:20:47.149
And on top of that, so the community and I
think the journalistic community as well is

00:20:47.149 --> 00:20:51.159
is aware of these issues and is is trying
things.

00:20:51.159 --> 00:20:55.289
Are there ways that we can get around this
and for example the journal development which

00:20:55.289 --> 00:20:59.619
is the big journal in my field has recently
announced that you can submit it in any form

00:20:59.619 --> 00:21:01.190
you want.

00:21:01.190 --> 00:21:02.700
And they will deal with it.

00:21:02.700 --> 00:21:07.320
They have decided that reviewers are capable
of reading a paper even if it's a different

00:21:07.320 --> 00:21:13.409
font than the one they...so that's great.

00:21:13.409 --> 00:21:14.409
That's a really nice thing.

00:21:14.409 --> 00:21:19.080
Other journals are working there there are
some journals now that are connected up so

00:21:19.080 --> 00:21:23.749
that if you don't get into the highest impact
one you can have it exactly you can have it

00:21:23.749 --> 00:21:27.460
submitted to the next one down without doing
anything else.

00:21:27.460 --> 00:21:30.559
Saving you time that you can spend on science.

00:21:30.559 --> 00:21:31.559
Exactly.

00:21:31.559 --> 00:21:32.559
So.

00:21:32.559 --> 00:21:36.999
So trying to mitigate some of this silliness
that we spend our time on.

00:21:36.999 --> 00:21:42.009
But even when you spend all this time I mean
if you look at the percentage of applications

00:21:42.009 --> 00:21:45.289
that are accepted and lead to a grant.

00:21:45.289 --> 00:21:46.289
Right.

00:21:46.289 --> 00:21:47.289
Yeah.

00:21:47.289 --> 00:21:50.190
I mean that's the other problem is is limited,
there are limited funds.

00:21:50.190 --> 00:21:52.649
And the competition the competition is intense.

00:21:52.649 --> 00:21:58.610
How does that affect the science that you
decide to do?

00:21:58.610 --> 00:22:02.840
So the big granting agencies the National
Institutes of Health for which there is 18

00:22:02.840 --> 00:22:06.239
I think and then and.

00:22:06.239 --> 00:22:10.869
By the way the more important problem I think
is National Science Foundation so NIH is biomedical.

00:22:10.869 --> 00:22:13.679
NSF it's everything else.

00:22:13.679 --> 00:22:19.249
Everything else and its budget is very small
compared to NIH's.

00:22:19.249 --> 00:22:20.470
But.

00:22:20.470 --> 00:22:21.690
You.

00:22:21.690 --> 00:22:27.289
So right now I think in my field it is the
places where I apply for money, you have to

00:22:27.289 --> 00:22:33.480
be in the top four or five percent of your
peers for people who will understand what

00:22:33.480 --> 00:22:34.480
you're trying to do.

00:22:34.480 --> 00:22:38.960
Rank it and then they decide and you have
to be in the top four percent.

00:22:38.960 --> 00:22:41.789
And if you're not you will lose, you know,
that's it.

00:22:41.789 --> 00:22:43.369
There goes your salary.

00:22:43.369 --> 00:22:44.369
And that's hard.

00:22:44.369 --> 00:22:49.269
You're competing with people across the entire
nation and to stay in the top four percent.

00:22:49.269 --> 00:22:52.690
It's one thing to be an A student in high
school.

00:22:52.690 --> 00:23:02.190
It's quite another to be the only A, the one
you know one A in thousands.

00:23:02.190 --> 00:23:08.279
And that's, so what you tend to do is you
you, these agencies what they do is they put

00:23:08.279 --> 00:23:13.999
out these things called RFA, requests for
applications, and these are where they're

00:23:13.999 --> 00:23:20.309
saying alright, Congress has decided that
what we should spend our money on this year

00:23:20.309 --> 00:23:22.799
is mapping the brain.

00:23:22.799 --> 00:23:26.409
And so they'll say they'll set aside OK we're
going to set aside $50 million for people

00:23:26.409 --> 00:23:30.779
who want to map the brain and then they put
out these RFAs and you go to the website and

00:23:30.779 --> 00:23:32.480
you think well OK I'm a brain person.

00:23:32.480 --> 00:23:35.299
I'm going to go and I'm going to find out.

00:23:35.299 --> 00:23:38.059
Is there money out there for doing this thing?

00:23:38.059 --> 00:23:44.380
Well it turns out there isn’t, it turns
out that some congress person got put in there

00:23:44.380 --> 00:23:46.989
that what really matters is this part of the
brain.

00:23:46.989 --> 00:23:50.820
You know if you're studying the cerebellum
you've got a much better chance but if you're

00:23:50.820 --> 00:23:54.049
studying the cortex we're just not interested
this year.

00:23:54.049 --> 00:23:56.749
So yeah you might very well switch.

00:23:56.749 --> 00:24:01.470
And so it ends up ultimately being guided
by Congress.

00:24:01.470 --> 00:24:05.299
It's up to Congress to say, this is what's
on the agenda now.

00:24:05.299 --> 00:24:11.710
This is what we think is important, and the
scientists are becoming more savvy about this.

00:24:11.710 --> 00:24:16.480
And there's a lot more scientists now going
to Washington and we now get trained in how

00:24:16.480 --> 00:24:24.349
to lobby for science because that it loops
back around to what you're going to be able,

00:24:24.349 --> 00:24:28.759
what what passion, are you going to be able
to follow your passion in a direction that

00:24:28.759 --> 00:24:34.200
you as the trained person think is the best
direction to go or are you going to have to

00:24:34.200 --> 00:24:38.590
fit it into something that came down from
Congress.

00:24:38.590 --> 00:24:45.590
How do you I mean how do you think about how
this fierce competition then affects the papers

00:24:45.590 --> 00:24:49.799
that get published and then get reported on?

00:24:49.799 --> 00:24:54.499
Do you see that do you see an influence happening
there?

00:24:54.499 --> 00:24:59.029
Couple of things that I'd like to add to what
you guys have been talking about for the last

00:24:59.029 --> 00:25:00.029
few minutes.

00:25:00.029 --> 00:25:05.210
So NSF in my field we're talking about the
funding rate is five to six percent.

00:25:05.210 --> 00:25:14.940
That means that when I was an active PI I
had to write a grant proposal every semester.

00:25:14.940 --> 00:25:19.989
And two out of three will probably be rejected
which still it was much higher than...

00:25:19.989 --> 00:25:21.049
That's a fabulous record.

00:25:21.049 --> 00:25:22.049
That’s really good.

00:25:22.049 --> 00:25:23.230
But that was frustrating.

00:25:23.230 --> 00:25:27.869
That was a lot of time then yes you're right
you learn also how to lobby.

00:25:27.869 --> 00:25:31.499
That's another chunk of time that goes away
from the science, do something that you are

00:25:31.499 --> 00:25:33.720
not really set up to do to begin with.

00:25:33.720 --> 00:25:36.100
But we have to realize that there's are a
couple of things going on.

00:25:36.100 --> 00:25:40.330
First of all this is actually fairly new and
also it's not unavoidable because there are

00:25:40.330 --> 00:25:41.859
other ways of doing things.

00:25:41.859 --> 00:25:46.609
So it's new in the sense that NSF and all
the major federal agencies were created after

00:25:46.609 --> 00:25:47.929
World War Two.

00:25:47.929 --> 00:25:50.969
And that's why Einstein didn't have to do
that sort of stuff or Bohr didn't have to

00:25:50.969 --> 00:25:54.190
do that sort of stuff because they were simply
not around.

00:25:54.190 --> 00:25:58.830
Even even after they were created initially
there was a flood of money after the war was

00:25:58.830 --> 00:26:01.590
you know in terms of economic expansion.

00:26:01.590 --> 00:26:05.990
There was always you know science, especially
physics had a lot of cache.

00:26:05.990 --> 00:26:08.659
And so they were just getting money.

00:26:08.659 --> 00:26:11.539
The as you said when you introduce me I'm
the K.D.

00:26:11.539 --> 00:26:14.259
Irani Professor Philosophy at City College,
well K.D.

00:26:14.259 --> 00:26:19.009
Irani was hired you know like four decades
ago at City College because Einstein met him

00:26:19.009 --> 00:26:21.470
at Princeton and he wrote to City College
and said, I like this guy.

00:26:21.470 --> 00:26:22.590
You should hire him.

00:26:22.590 --> 00:26:24.269
And he was hired.

00:26:24.269 --> 00:26:26.230
That I guarantee you that's not going to happen
today.

00:26:26.230 --> 00:26:27.479
It's not going to happen.

00:26:27.479 --> 00:26:32.149
Turned out to be a good decision but nonetheless.

00:26:32.149 --> 00:26:36.299
So all of these are actually fairly new and
it's gotten much much worse over the last

00:26:36.299 --> 00:26:38.950
20, 30 years or so.

00:26:38.950 --> 00:26:44.019
But there's also some very bizarre ways that
actually typical of the American system that

00:26:44.019 --> 00:26:48.480
you don't necessarily find in other countries
in the world, even in other Western countries.

00:26:48.480 --> 00:26:54.029
So NSF for instance for a long time has concentrated
more and more money on a smaller smaller number

00:26:54.029 --> 00:26:55.029
of laboratories.

00:26:55.029 --> 00:26:56.269
They go for the big project.

00:26:56.269 --> 00:26:57.539
They go for the big things.

00:26:57.539 --> 00:27:00.940
One of the reasons is as you were saying a
minute ago because that sells to Congress.

00:27:00.940 --> 00:27:05.590
And I can tell you a story that occurred a
few years ago that I thought was hilarious.

00:27:05.590 --> 00:27:07.970
And at the same time extremely sad.

00:27:07.970 --> 00:27:12.820
So I was invited to a NSF retreat with a bunch
of other ecologists and biologists.

00:27:12.820 --> 00:27:16.849
They told us, look we want to give you big
money.

00:27:16.849 --> 00:27:21.159
But the way to give you big money is to attach
it to some kind of big project because otherwise

00:27:21.159 --> 00:27:23.809
Congress is not going to do it.

00:27:23.809 --> 00:27:25.919
And so they said you know try to tell us.

00:27:25.919 --> 00:27:27.610
Think about it for this entire weekend.

00:27:27.610 --> 00:27:32.820
Think about the equivalent in ecology of let's
say on an astronomical observatory or a satellite

00:27:32.820 --> 00:27:35.359
or something like that or a particle accelerator.

00:27:35.359 --> 00:27:40.269
So let's say that we want to give you not
the usual 500,000, 600,000 dollars but you

00:27:40.269 --> 00:27:41.570
know 30 million dollars.

00:27:41.570 --> 00:27:42.690
What would you do with that?

00:27:42.690 --> 00:27:49.279
And it was hilarious because the answer was
I could hire 200 post docs because most of

00:27:49.279 --> 00:27:51.149
them work in colleges' field work.

00:27:51.149 --> 00:27:52.729
I don't need an observatory or a satellite.

00:27:52.729 --> 00:27:56.649
I do need however a lot of people in the field
doing the actual work but that isn't the kind

00:27:56.649 --> 00:27:59.799
of thing that is selling the project to Congress.

00:27:59.799 --> 00:28:03.710
Other countries do it differently although
unfortunately more and more they're following

00:28:03.710 --> 00:28:07.601
the American model because America is capable
of exporting a lot of good things and then

00:28:07.601 --> 00:28:11.419
all those bad things and the rest of world
somehow buys it.

00:28:11.419 --> 00:28:17.529
So Canada and most Europe for instance, they
do a different system where they give smaller

00:28:17.529 --> 00:28:20.929
grants to a much larger number of labs.

00:28:20.929 --> 00:28:25.119
And the way it works is you write much smaller
proposals instead of a 15-page proposal for

00:28:25.119 --> 00:28:28.889
NSF, you have you know three or four pages
proposal and all you have to do is really

00:28:28.889 --> 00:28:31.200
to maintain a certain publication record.

00:28:31.200 --> 00:28:35.559
If you can show that over the last three years
you have published a decent number of papers

00:28:35.559 --> 00:28:39.649
in decent journals and then you now have a
new idea you want to proceed in that direction.

00:28:39.649 --> 00:28:41.950
They're going to look at it and go, yeah sure.

00:28:41.950 --> 00:28:42.950
Good enough.

00:28:42.950 --> 00:28:44.779
That's we're going to give you the money and
we'll see how it goes.

00:28:44.779 --> 00:28:46.179
You know three years down the road.

00:28:46.179 --> 00:28:49.219
That's not the case for NSF and things like
that.

00:28:49.219 --> 00:28:52.929
So there are different ways of structuring
the system and I think part of the problem

00:28:52.929 --> 00:28:57.219
is that of course the American system is the
one that does pump more money than anybody

00:28:57.219 --> 00:29:04.022
else in scientific research and so we think
of science in the way in which it's done in

00:29:04.022 --> 00:29:05.022
the United States.

00:29:05.022 --> 00:29:06.022
But it's not the only model.

00:29:06.022 --> 00:29:07.022
So what's your perspective on it?

00:29:07.022 --> 00:29:12.440
I mean if it maybe pumps more money than many
others but the sad fact about U.S. research

00:29:12.440 --> 00:29:17.649
is that the funding overall has been roughly
static since the 1980s and down.

00:29:17.649 --> 00:29:20.460
And I actually get quite concerned.

00:29:20.460 --> 00:29:25.779
I like science that provides solutions as
much as the next person because we know one

00:29:25.779 --> 00:29:30.820
of the challenges and why Nobel Prizes take
so long is basic research may take decades

00:29:30.820 --> 00:29:32.320
to then get to applied.

00:29:32.320 --> 00:29:37.249
And surely nobody, surely Einstein who was
mentioned earlier today and this is a this

00:29:37.249 --> 00:29:40.219
is something that maybe a lot of folks in
the audience have heard already, certainly

00:29:40.219 --> 00:29:44.289
when he was working on relativity theories
there was never thinking about it being applied

00:29:44.289 --> 00:29:48.099
in my iPhone with my GPS which of course we
do every day without thinking.

00:29:48.099 --> 00:29:52.909
But even the iPhones have a couple of dozen
innovations in them themselves that nobody

00:29:52.909 --> 00:29:55.340
thought of would be applied later on.

00:29:55.340 --> 00:29:57.460
This is one challenge I see.

00:29:57.460 --> 00:30:03.059
Some interesting models I also like are places
where in Europe, I think there's some great

00:30:03.059 --> 00:30:07.179
leadership on this, funding the researcher
rather than the research project which I think

00:30:07.179 --> 00:30:08.179
is encouraging.

00:30:08.179 --> 00:30:09.210
So how would that work?

00:30:09.210 --> 00:30:14.690
So a researcher has demonstrated through some
initial work over time that they have some

00:30:14.690 --> 00:30:18.690
interesting lines of inquiry that they will
be pursuing and they get a block of money

00:30:18.690 --> 00:30:22.679
for maybe five years at a time instead of
one study the next study, the next study.

00:30:22.679 --> 00:30:24.190
I think that's quite interesting.

00:30:24.190 --> 00:30:27.320
I think that's one way to go about it.

00:30:27.320 --> 00:30:33.119
Another is for the publication piece of it
you might decide that the publication will

00:30:33.119 --> 00:30:39.179
be OK as long as the methods and statistical
accuracy that you're going to use for analyses

00:30:39.179 --> 00:30:40.549
are are agreed beforehand.

00:30:40.549 --> 00:30:45.369
And one of our relatively newer journals Nature
Human Behavior is looking at this sort of

00:30:45.369 --> 00:30:50.099
model as well so that the researchers know
when they've submitted something we can kind

00:30:50.099 --> 00:30:54.529
of pre peer review almost that tells them
that when they get when they get done through

00:30:54.529 --> 00:30:56.460
it they will get to publish something.

00:30:56.460 --> 00:31:03.509
So models like that and also I'm thinking
broad, MIT and Harvard and how they have staff

00:31:03.509 --> 00:31:09.479
scientists to help work and remove some of
that admin burden that many of the researchers

00:31:09.479 --> 00:31:10.479
are shouldering.

00:31:10.479 --> 00:31:15.809
You mentioned about grants, and you listening
to people as they're futzing through the grants.

00:31:15.809 --> 00:31:20.269
I think the long story short of it is that
there are so many elements that challenge

00:31:20.269 --> 00:31:24.509
research and we're going to have to take kind
of a multi-pronged approach to solving all

00:31:24.509 --> 00:31:26.149
of these various problems.

00:31:26.149 --> 00:31:28.349
I want to switch gears a little bit.

00:31:28.349 --> 00:31:33.489
Ivan, maybe you could tell us a little bit
about Retraction Watch.

00:31:33.489 --> 00:31:39.169
I want to sort of talk about some of the things
that with the panel as a whole that some of

00:31:39.169 --> 00:31:44.419
the things that you kind of you know expose
as you as you run this site.

00:31:44.419 --> 00:31:45.419
So.

00:31:45.419 --> 00:31:50.979
So why, you know as a journalist why would
you say hey you know what I'm going to do

00:31:50.979 --> 00:31:57.519
I'm going to set up a web site dedicated only
to retractions of scientific studies.

00:31:57.519 --> 00:32:00.860
Like why why would you do that?

00:32:00.860 --> 00:32:01.860
That's a great question Carl.

00:32:01.860 --> 00:32:05.729
If I had the answer I probably wouldn't have
started it.

00:32:05.729 --> 00:32:07.190
So Adam Marcus is my co-founder.

00:32:07.190 --> 00:32:10.749
He and I launched Retraction Watch not quite
seven years ago now, it’ll be seven years

00:32:10.749 --> 00:32:11.749
in August.

00:32:11.749 --> 00:32:17.830
And to be honest the very basic idea there
were really two guiding principles or sort

00:32:17.830 --> 00:32:21.059
of reasons why we were interested in doing
this.

00:32:21.059 --> 00:32:24.360
In particular Adam had been reporting on retractions
for a while.

00:32:24.360 --> 00:32:28.869
I'd been doing the same but credit where credit
is due, he had broken a couple of big stories.

00:32:28.869 --> 00:32:34.209
One particular and it's a anesthesiology researcher
who ended up going to prison because of scientific

00:32:34.209 --> 00:32:35.209
fraud.

00:32:35.209 --> 00:32:37.109
It's a very rare phenomena, very rare event.

00:32:37.109 --> 00:32:38.950
But that of course grabbed everyone's attention.

00:32:38.950 --> 00:32:43.609
In fact I was at Scientific American at the
time and we reported on this after Adam did

00:32:43.609 --> 00:32:44.629
and all that.

00:32:44.629 --> 00:32:49.049
And so one, that sort of speaks to the fact
that often, scientific retractions, maybe

00:32:49.049 --> 00:32:50.999
I'll just back up and explain what they are.

00:32:50.999 --> 00:32:51.999
Sure.

00:32:51.999 --> 00:32:55.929
Because I think that many people including
me until about seven years ago it wasn't quite

00:32:55.929 --> 00:32:56.929
clear.

00:32:56.929 --> 00:33:00.799
This is sort of if you will the and I'm going
to mix metaphors, I definitely shouldn't do

00:33:00.799 --> 00:33:07.379
it with Massimo here but this is the nuclear
option if you will of self-correction or any

00:33:07.379 --> 00:33:08.789
kind of correction in science.

00:33:08.789 --> 00:33:13.109
So you're saying not only is there, was there
a problem with this this paper, this finding

00:33:13.109 --> 00:33:16.049
maybe there's an error here but you shouldn't
trust it.

00:33:16.049 --> 00:33:18.669
You shouldn't rely on it all.

00:33:18.669 --> 00:33:22.289
You shouldn't cite it or if you do cite it
you should make it very clear that it's been

00:33:22.289 --> 00:33:23.289
retracted.

00:33:23.289 --> 00:33:26.850
It is again, retracted from the literature,
withdrawn from the literature.

00:33:26.850 --> 00:33:30.239
Guidelines say it should still sit there in
the literature review marked really almost

00:33:30.239 --> 00:33:34.989
with a scarlet letter, in fact often a red
stamp that says retracted on it.

00:33:34.989 --> 00:33:38.879
And so that's what retraction is and what
Adam started seeing with these retractions

00:33:38.879 --> 00:33:43.109
from this particular anesthesiologist was
wow there's a great big story here.

00:33:43.109 --> 00:33:48.349
And as a journalist I mean there are investigative
journalists who I admire and just want to

00:33:48.349 --> 00:33:52.750
be one one day but really Adam and I think
of ourselves as finding stories that are hiding

00:33:52.750 --> 00:33:55.959
in plain sight and retractions are a great
example of that.

00:33:55.959 --> 00:33:56.959
So here's a bunch of retractions.

00:33:56.959 --> 00:33:57.959
They seem very arcane.

00:33:57.959 --> 00:33:59.219
Why is this paper being retracted?

00:33:59.219 --> 00:34:04.629
Oh wait a second this guy's made up all the
data for 25 different studies.

00:34:04.629 --> 00:34:08.960
So there's that line at the end of a lot of
Hollywood movies you know, no no animals were

00:34:08.960 --> 00:34:13.409
harmed in the making of this film and so it
was sort of like that no patients were harmed

00:34:13.409 --> 00:34:17.049
in the making of this study because there
weren't actually any patients in this study.

00:34:17.049 --> 00:34:18.879
So that's sort of a different take.

00:34:18.879 --> 00:34:20.019
So there was that element.

00:34:20.019 --> 00:34:23.339
But the other thing we noticed was that the
retraction notices themselves.

00:34:23.339 --> 00:34:24.899
This is not a very transparent process.

00:34:24.899 --> 00:34:26.490
I mean the process is not transparent.

00:34:26.490 --> 00:34:29.090
The outcome is not transparent.

00:34:29.090 --> 00:34:33.760
So the retraction notices themselves were
either, you know, some of them actually told

00:34:33.760 --> 00:34:37.159
you what happened but a lot of them either
they were sort of mealy mouthed.

00:34:37.159 --> 00:34:40.599
They wouldn't really tell you the whole story
or they would actually be wrong.

00:34:40.599 --> 00:34:44.649
They would make something sound like it was
an honest error when in fact it was fraudulent.

00:34:44.649 --> 00:34:50.720
And it turns out we've now learned you know
few years after we started the blog that A)

00:34:50.720 --> 00:34:57.049
the number of retractions has dramatically
increased, it's far outpaced the amount of

00:34:57.049 --> 00:35:00.950
papers being published, there’s a rate of
growth in that, think when you and I one of

00:35:00.950 --> 00:35:04.460
the times we talked for the Times about a
study that came out showing that, the number

00:35:04.460 --> 00:35:09.769
of retractions went up tenfold from about
40 per year in 2000 to 400 per year in 2010

00:35:09.769 --> 00:35:10.810
and it's continued to grow.

00:35:10.810 --> 00:35:16.559
That's out of about one and a half to two
million papers so it's still a very rare event.

00:35:16.559 --> 00:35:19.569
And you know the other thing that's happened
is that we know that now two thirds of them

00:35:19.569 --> 00:35:21.599
are due to misconduct or due to fraud.

00:35:21.599 --> 00:35:27.119
So there's the sort of US federal definition
of fraud and it is or misconduct misconduct

00:35:27.119 --> 00:35:31.870
which is something that has also been sort
of exported to other countries in a lot of,

00:35:31.870 --> 00:35:38.990
a lot of agreement on that but it's FFP is
the, FFP is the, what do you call it, the

00:35:38.990 --> 00:35:39.990
acronym.

00:35:39.990 --> 00:35:43.380
So it's falsification, fabrication and plagiarism.

00:35:43.380 --> 00:35:45.970
Plagiarism hopefully is self-evident to everyone
in the room.

00:35:45.970 --> 00:35:48.609
But we can talk later if it isn't.

00:35:48.609 --> 00:35:54.440
But falsification is you did an experiment
but it didn't go exactly the way you think

00:35:54.440 --> 00:35:59.740
it should have gone to prove the sort of whatever
it is that you're trying to prove.

00:35:59.740 --> 00:36:02.369
So you fudged the results a little bit.

00:36:02.369 --> 00:36:06.609
Maybe you literally used Photoshop and you
take out that little band that shouldn't have

00:36:06.609 --> 00:36:07.890
been there, a little spot.

00:36:07.890 --> 00:36:11.730
Oh I'm sure that was just an error, you know
the the lab tech didn't have enough coffee

00:36:11.730 --> 00:36:13.990
that morning and so I'll just erase that one.

00:36:13.990 --> 00:36:14.990
That was an outlier.

00:36:14.990 --> 00:36:16.310
The ultimate in confirmation bias.

00:36:16.310 --> 00:36:17.789
That's complete confirmation bias.

00:36:17.789 --> 00:36:19.430
I know I'm right.

00:36:19.430 --> 00:36:21.769
I'll just treat the photograph just so everyone
knows it.

00:36:21.769 --> 00:36:25.500
Right, and then the other half is fabrication
where you actually make it up like this guy

00:36:25.500 --> 00:36:27.089
Scott Reuben did.

00:36:27.089 --> 00:36:28.309
And so that's the sort of triad.

00:36:28.309 --> 00:36:31.420
We've seen a lot of other things happening.

00:36:31.420 --> 00:36:34.660
Crazy stories about people doing their own
peer review.

00:36:34.660 --> 00:36:37.910
Actually manage to do the peer review of their
own papers.

00:36:37.910 --> 00:36:38.910
I'm sure you're all familiar with peer reviews.

00:36:38.910 --> 00:36:40.910
The idea is that it's been done by a peer.

00:36:40.910 --> 00:36:46.160
Now I happen to think that I'm peerless so
there are no peers, but you actually are supposed

00:36:46.160 --> 00:36:48.119
to have someone else do that.

00:36:48.119 --> 00:36:49.630
It turns out…

00:36:49.630 --> 00:36:51.990
It’s not a reflexive property.

00:36:51.990 --> 00:36:57.430
So there have been just about 500 papers retracted
because people figured out a way to either

00:36:57.430 --> 00:37:01.559
peer review the paper themselves or have someone
sort of do it for them so that they could

00:37:01.559 --> 00:37:05.260
then peer review their paper and not in the
sort of usual way that we all know that happens.

00:37:05.260 --> 00:37:08.020
But in a directed way.

00:37:08.020 --> 00:37:09.020
So.

00:37:09.020 --> 00:37:20.359
Mariette, what's your feeling on sort of you
know the issue with retracted papers and you

00:37:20.359 --> 00:37:21.359
know perhaps reflecting these these pressures
were talking about?

00:37:21.359 --> 00:37:25.200
Certainly acknowledging all the pressures
and actually I just want to put in you know

00:37:25.200 --> 00:37:30.359
praise for for Retraction Watch and for scientists
who, you know let's not forget science is

00:37:30.359 --> 00:37:31.359
a process.

00:37:31.359 --> 00:37:35.960
I talked a little earlier about how we sometimes
in school in early school years treat it as

00:37:35.960 --> 00:37:38.410
you know a series of received wisdom.

00:37:38.410 --> 00:37:41.599
Bits of facts that everybody knows and that
that's always true.

00:37:41.599 --> 00:37:46.029
But actually one of the things that's very
wonderful about science is that it tries to

00:37:46.029 --> 00:37:50.289
embrace its corrections and self corrections
and so on.

00:37:50.289 --> 00:37:56.339
And I also thought listening to to your excellent
outline Ivan that a thing we should mention

00:37:56.339 --> 00:38:00.880
for folks who may be not familiar is that
you know the fact that you can reproduce something

00:38:00.880 --> 00:38:02.420
doesn't make it right.

00:38:02.420 --> 00:38:05.130
The fact that you can't reproduce it doesn't
make it wrong.

00:38:05.130 --> 00:38:08.200
There are lots of things that happen in between.

00:38:08.200 --> 00:38:12.430
Problems with your reagents, problems with
the techniques, with the methods used in the

00:38:12.430 --> 00:38:13.430
lab.

00:38:13.430 --> 00:38:18.430
And this is why I talked a little earlier
about what publishing outlets like mine can

00:38:18.430 --> 00:38:23.900
help do, for one thing in our in our journals
they remove the restrictions they used to

00:38:23.900 --> 00:38:25.930
have on length of methods.

00:38:25.930 --> 00:38:30.490
We were talking about fitting a couple of
words on a page we used to have restrictions

00:38:30.490 --> 00:38:35.119
and now online they can be much longer and
provide more detail so other researchers can

00:38:35.119 --> 00:38:39.910
can help provide that course correction or
correction by checking something.

00:38:39.910 --> 00:38:44.650
Or providing protocols or providing the raw
data which we now increasingly hook to the

00:38:44.650 --> 00:38:46.210
figures that are in the papers as well.

00:38:46.210 --> 00:38:52.260
All of these help the scientific communities
police themselves in addition to you know.

00:38:52.260 --> 00:38:55.690
You're quite right there's of course there
is wrongdoing and science is ultimately done

00:38:55.690 --> 00:38:56.690
by humans.

00:38:56.690 --> 00:39:01.280
And they are flawed every so often but there
are these larger factors at play as well.

00:39:01.280 --> 00:39:02.280
Right.

00:39:02.280 --> 00:39:08.099
And even if even if people are not committing
fraud or misconduct or fabrication.

00:39:08.099 --> 00:39:15.309
I mean there's also the issue of do you have
have you have your students challenge themselves

00:39:15.309 --> 00:39:16.309
enough?

00:39:16.309 --> 00:39:21.319
Or you know as I said like whoa I've got the
value I want, got to go publish, you know

00:39:21.319 --> 00:39:22.880
rather than take…

00:39:22.880 --> 00:39:23.880
P-hacking…

00:39:23.880 --> 00:39:26.430
Known as p-hacking, right exactly.

00:39:26.430 --> 00:39:31.420
And so I mean do you see that as a pressure
like you know like.

00:39:31.420 --> 00:39:37.640
I mean maybe young scientists don't have the
luxury of spending another year or two saying

00:39:37.640 --> 00:39:39.330
that I don't think that's quite right.

00:39:39.330 --> 00:39:43.150
Let me make sure that my results are good.

00:39:43.150 --> 00:39:45.240
Couple of comments to some things I've heard
here.

00:39:45.240 --> 00:39:52.519
One is that another one of the perhaps unique
things about science is you don't have to

00:39:52.519 --> 00:39:53.519
be right.

00:39:53.519 --> 00:39:54.940
How do you mean?

00:39:54.940 --> 00:39:56.030
So, science meanders.

00:39:56.030 --> 00:39:59.789
I love the title of this salon.

00:39:59.789 --> 00:40:09.960
An adjunct to that point is that the way scientific
paper scientific studies are reported, the

00:40:09.960 --> 00:40:15.470
structure of a scientific paper: an introduction,
a section that describes how you did it, a

00:40:15.470 --> 00:40:20.400
section that describes what you got and then
there's a whole section called the discussion

00:40:20.400 --> 00:40:25.750
where you put in what you think it means and
you may be wrong and that's fine.

00:40:25.750 --> 00:40:27.780
That's not the criterion.

00:40:27.780 --> 00:40:31.490
The criterion is, did you do the right thing?

00:40:31.490 --> 00:40:32.490
Did you answer that?

00:40:32.490 --> 00:40:33.490
Did you do the right controls?

00:40:33.490 --> 00:40:36.570
Did you use your techniques properly?

00:40:36.570 --> 00:40:41.019
And the results, this was described to me
when I was a student, as the results is the

00:40:41.019 --> 00:40:44.170
part of the paper that will never change.

00:40:44.170 --> 00:40:48.609
When you did exactly what you have said in
the method and material section, is supposed

00:40:48.609 --> 00:40:54.230
to be where you provide enough detail that
someone can replicate it.

00:40:54.230 --> 00:40:55.230
And then there's the results.

00:40:55.230 --> 00:41:00.430
That's what happened in that situation on
those days with those reagents and dadadada.

00:41:00.430 --> 00:41:05.460
And it's one of the things I think that makes
these papers very boring because, you know

00:41:05.460 --> 00:41:06.460
what's the fun part?

00:41:06.460 --> 00:41:07.589
The fun part is what does it mean?

00:41:07.589 --> 00:41:08.589
What does it tell me?

00:41:08.589 --> 00:41:11.190
How do I interpret this?

00:41:11.190 --> 00:41:13.160
But it's absolutely critical.

00:41:13.160 --> 00:41:18.290
And I'm not the the person who's going to
figure out how to do this...

00:41:18.290 --> 00:41:24.019
But that's the stuff that gets lost when you
switch to journalism.

00:41:24.019 --> 00:41:26.900
Where you need to have a headline.

00:41:26.900 --> 00:41:31.160
Where you need to sell a paper and you have
exactly two inches for your headline.

00:41:31.160 --> 00:41:37.590
And it's... but the point I wanted to make
is that this is the structure of the paper

00:41:37.590 --> 00:41:44.200
that convention that tradition is another
one of the ways that scientists police.

00:41:44.200 --> 00:41:46.350
Is it going to come out right?

00:41:46.350 --> 00:41:49.130
You say here's what I did and it's open game.

00:41:49.130 --> 00:41:54.220
Anybody who wants to and who has the money
and equipment can replicate it and disagree

00:41:54.220 --> 00:41:59.819
with you and you have this whole section that's
your your opinion and it's separated out and

00:41:59.819 --> 00:42:02.480
it's this is the editorial part.

00:42:02.480 --> 00:42:04.059
Here's what happened.

00:42:04.059 --> 00:42:05.769
Here's the editorial part.

00:42:05.769 --> 00:42:07.310
And and we build on each other.

00:42:07.310 --> 00:42:12.019
We don't have one person who's studied, you
know, we have Newton and we have these wonderful

00:42:12.019 --> 00:42:14.329
laws and that's great.

00:42:14.329 --> 00:42:16.990
And he was wrong.

00:42:16.990 --> 00:42:24.210
And it's, he was he was wrong when you try
to then apply them in other circumstances.

00:42:24.210 --> 00:42:28.180
So but but science meanders and science builds.

00:42:28.180 --> 00:42:32.240
And and of course you end up you know you
want your grant.

00:42:32.240 --> 00:42:33.420
You want your headline.

00:42:33.420 --> 00:42:39.310
You want those things but there are so many
little places built in to try to keep us honest.

00:42:39.310 --> 00:42:43.869
To some extent these are now a question of
incentives and incentives can be structured

00:42:43.869 --> 00:42:46.079
again differently.

00:42:46.079 --> 00:42:50.359
So for instance we talk a lot about replication
but of course there's no incentive whatsoever

00:42:50.359 --> 00:42:51.940
to replicate somebody else's results.

00:42:51.940 --> 00:42:57.519
Why why why so explain why wouldn't scientists
want to spend their time trying to replicate

00:42:57.519 --> 00:42:58.539
what someone else did?

00:42:58.539 --> 00:42:59.569
Because it's only the adventure right.

00:42:59.569 --> 00:43:01.369
You want to be the first one to get there.

00:43:01.369 --> 00:43:04.170
Not the second one or the third one or the
fourth one.

00:43:04.170 --> 00:43:05.200
And you can't publish it.

00:43:05.200 --> 00:43:06.279
Usually you don't publish it.

00:43:06.279 --> 00:43:07.609
Well why can't you publish it?

00:43:07.609 --> 00:43:08.609
I mean…

00:43:08.609 --> 00:43:12.980
Because most journals especially the high
impact journals want the novel stuff, the

00:43:12.980 --> 00:43:15.210
sexy stuff, the things that nobody's done
before.

00:43:15.210 --> 00:43:16.210
Right?

00:43:16.210 --> 00:43:18.119
So even though that's slowly beginning to
change.

00:43:18.119 --> 00:43:22.480
I mean there are some journals who accept
replications but those are not the ones that

00:43:22.480 --> 00:43:23.650
you build your career you on.

00:43:23.650 --> 00:43:26.950
You don't become famous as a scientist by
saying yeah yeah I checked these other the

00:43:26.950 --> 00:43:27.950
guys work.

00:43:27.950 --> 00:43:29.380
That was correct.

00:43:29.380 --> 00:43:30.380
Nobody.

00:43:30.380 --> 00:43:33.460
It just doesn't pay to arrive as number two
or three or four.

00:43:33.460 --> 00:43:37.769
So that's one thing in terms of incentive
structure.

00:43:37.769 --> 00:43:43.440
There's also the issue that the publish or
perish sort of culture arguably is always

00:43:43.440 --> 00:43:48.650
been there at least since World War Two but
it has gotten worse precisely because there

00:43:48.650 --> 00:43:52.059
is a large number of scientists, a large number
of journals.

00:43:52.059 --> 00:43:53.539
How many journal do you guys publish?

00:43:53.539 --> 00:43:56.061
At Science Nature it's about 2,000.

00:43:56.061 --> 00:43:59.400
So you alone, Science Nature, publishes 2,000
journals.

00:43:59.400 --> 00:44:00.690
That's one publishing house.

00:44:00.690 --> 00:44:01.690
Right.

00:44:01.690 --> 00:44:05.700
So the result of it is that you know you were
saying earlier if somebody publishes a paper

00:44:05.700 --> 00:44:08.069
and nobody reads it or cites it the, you know,
what was that.

00:44:08.069 --> 00:44:12.390
Well the thing that really opened my eyes
is a few years ago there was a study that

00:44:12.390 --> 00:44:18.470
showed that of all the papers published in
major pop biology journals, the really top

00:44:18.470 --> 00:44:24.809
notch journals, one third only is cited within
five years from publication.

00:44:24.809 --> 00:44:30.420
That means two thirds, let's get let's get
this straight like two thirds of top papers,

00:44:30.420 --> 00:44:35.529
published papers, published in top journals
never get cited period.

00:44:35.529 --> 00:44:37.430
That's a lot of waste of money and energy.

00:44:37.430 --> 00:44:42.019
The question though is, I'm sorry to interrupt
you but should citations, I mean citations

00:44:42.019 --> 00:44:45.480
obviously there are many alternative metrics
as well.

00:44:45.480 --> 00:44:47.040
Citation is important.

00:44:47.040 --> 00:44:51.970
You know publishers also do have places increasingly
now for publishing negative results like a

00:44:51.970 --> 00:44:56.481
scientific reports in my own journal group
will allow that kind of publication as help

00:44:56.481 --> 00:44:58.170
for science at the time.

00:44:58.170 --> 00:45:00.200
So incentives is a great point.

00:45:00.200 --> 00:45:01.200
Yeah.

00:45:01.200 --> 00:45:03.890
So you can change the structure of the of
the incentives.

00:45:03.890 --> 00:45:07.549
So one of the things that would that some
people including my colleagues and I've been

00:45:07.549 --> 00:45:12.430
doing when we got these 150 applications for
instance from young people, post docs, graduate

00:45:12.430 --> 00:45:17.020
students that want a new job instead of doing
the bean counting and say OK so how many papers

00:45:17.020 --> 00:45:20.089
you have, we say OK well that's nice.

00:45:20.089 --> 00:45:25.490
You've published 20 papers in the last five
years now pick your top three.

00:45:25.490 --> 00:45:29.220
The ones that you've got, not the ones that
have been published the best three journals

00:45:29.220 --> 00:45:33.760
the top two the ones you think you made an
impact and I'll read them.

00:45:33.760 --> 00:45:37.240
And the rest I'm just going to ignore because
it's just bean counting.

00:45:37.240 --> 00:45:42.410
That I think brings back a little bit of quality
into quality control into it instead of just

00:45:42.410 --> 00:45:43.410
doing the simple thing...

00:45:43.410 --> 00:45:48.170
Rather than just churning out one paper after
another and just measuring by the pound.

00:45:48.170 --> 00:45:53.089
And then I mean Adam and I have applauded
efforts like that because it actually gets

00:45:53.089 --> 00:45:56.670
back to let's read the paper and let's understand
what he....

00:45:56.670 --> 00:46:00.089
And who cares what... you could almost blind
yourself to what journal it’s in.

00:46:00.089 --> 00:46:01.980
With all due respect to Nature.

00:46:01.980 --> 00:46:06.650
I mean Nature Springer has every range, top
to bottom, in terms of...

00:46:06.650 --> 00:46:07.970
I don't mean bottom.

00:46:07.970 --> 00:46:11.990
But you know a range of selective or perhaps
less.

00:46:11.990 --> 00:46:14.539
From highly selective to sound science.

00:46:14.539 --> 00:46:20.250
But if I can be just sort of dark for a second
and I just because to put things in perspective,

00:46:20.250 --> 00:46:24.450
you know we're talking you know you're talking
about you know an American university.

00:46:24.450 --> 00:46:28.100
I think in Western Europe you see a lot of
the you know a lot of movements to do that

00:46:28.100 --> 00:46:32.759
and Mariette was talking about some different
funding models that are I would argue progressive

00:46:32.759 --> 00:46:34.329
in terms of that.

00:46:34.329 --> 00:46:37.500
Certain parts of the world are going in exactly
the opposite direction.

00:46:37.500 --> 00:46:45.079
So for example in China you get a half salary
bonus if you publish in a journal with a certain

00:46:45.079 --> 00:46:46.079
impact factor.

00:46:46.079 --> 00:46:47.849
It depends on your field and what have you.

00:46:47.849 --> 00:46:48.849
You know...

00:46:48.849 --> 00:46:51.099
So you get a paper in Nature you're going
to…

00:46:51.099 --> 00:46:52.099
Nature?

00:46:52.099 --> 00:46:53.099
Forget it.

00:46:53.099 --> 00:46:54.099
I think they'd buy you a palace.

00:46:54.099 --> 00:46:57.650
But I'm talking about you know a sort of a
respectable..

00:46:57.650 --> 00:46:58.650
OK, respectable.

00:46:58.650 --> 00:47:03.020
You get a paper in, just a respectable journal
and you're going to get six months of salary.

00:47:03.020 --> 00:47:04.200
Is that what you're saying?

00:47:04.200 --> 00:47:06.220
Right, and you can keep hitting the pinball
you know.

00:47:06.220 --> 00:47:09.960
I don't know if that metaphor works anymore
today but you can keep hitting that you know

00:47:09.960 --> 00:47:10.960
as many times.

00:47:10.960 --> 00:47:12.390
I'm sure there's some limit what have you.

00:47:12.390 --> 00:47:16.549
The point is I mean I like to think of myself
as a fairly you know I'm going to do what's

00:47:16.549 --> 00:47:18.480
right and sort of all ethical person.

00:47:18.480 --> 00:47:23.160
I've got to tell you if I you know was offered
the opportunity and had to figure out a way

00:47:23.160 --> 00:47:24.819
to do it, I might sort of.

00:47:24.819 --> 00:47:27.359
And even if I thought well I'm still doing
the right thing.

00:47:27.359 --> 00:47:30.039
It's going to it's going to clearly do something.

00:47:30.039 --> 00:47:35.540
And every country is somewhere on that spectrum
and you know even you know some for example

00:47:35.540 --> 00:47:37.160
in the UK there's that and it's changed a
bit.

00:47:37.160 --> 00:47:41.720
So this being maybe a little bit unfair but
there are these sort of framework exercises,

00:47:41.720 --> 00:47:46.670
exercise frameworks where you look at, evaluation
frameworks, excuse me, where departments get

00:47:46.670 --> 00:47:51.839
more money or your university gets more money
from the government if you have an average

00:47:51.839 --> 00:47:54.470
impact factor in a certain number of papers
and this sort of thing.

00:47:54.470 --> 00:47:58.480
So I applaud all of the efforts that people
are making to get away from that.

00:47:58.480 --> 00:48:02.410
I think we just have to sort of look at this
globally and understand what's happening.

00:48:02.410 --> 00:48:08.869
So I just want before we take some questions
I just wanted to switch it over a bit more

00:48:08.869 --> 00:48:14.900
and more to focus more on sort of like that
next step from the from the journal to the

00:48:14.900 --> 00:48:22.349
rest of the world like as stuff spreads out
through the news, through the magazines, the

00:48:22.349 --> 00:48:25.079
newspapers, online and so.

00:48:25.079 --> 00:48:29.529
Are are we sort of like on the outside of
this sort of dynamic?

00:48:29.529 --> 00:48:32.599
Do we have nothing to do with it or are we
kind of.

00:48:32.599 --> 00:48:37.329
Are we are we part of the problem?

00:48:37.329 --> 00:48:38.359
We can get to the solution later.

00:48:38.359 --> 00:48:39.589
Are we part of the problem?

00:48:39.589 --> 00:48:40.640
We, who?

00:48:40.640 --> 00:48:46.440
We, journalists…We will get confessions
from journalists first and then the scientists

00:48:46.440 --> 00:48:47.440
can...

00:48:47.440 --> 00:48:48.440
Just like the spectrum of anything.

00:48:48.440 --> 00:48:49.440
Yeah, sure.

00:48:49.440 --> 00:48:51.490
Many of us are click bait chasers.

00:48:51.490 --> 00:48:54.529
Many of us are publishing the latest headline.

00:48:54.529 --> 00:48:55.529
Many of us are....

00:48:55.529 --> 00:48:58.119
So we're going to look for that paper with
the flashiest claim.

00:48:58.119 --> 00:48:59.410
Yeah, it happens.

00:48:59.410 --> 00:49:01.800
I'd be lying if I said it didn't.

00:49:01.800 --> 00:49:06.940
How do we approach it, those of us who have
the privilege of you know in the case of Scientific

00:49:06.940 --> 00:49:10.049
American, let me put that that on as editor-in-chief
there.

00:49:10.049 --> 00:49:13.569
One of the first things I did when I became
editor-in-chief of Scientific American half

00:49:13.569 --> 00:49:20.569
a dozen years ago was instead of having the
scientists write more in that received wisdom

00:49:20.569 --> 00:49:24.650
kind of way which I was describing earlier
applied to education.

00:49:24.650 --> 00:49:29.831
But we used to have a science or one of the
things I should have said earlier, in case

00:49:29.831 --> 00:49:33.859
people don't know, is about half maybe more
of feature articles in Scientific American

00:49:33.859 --> 00:49:34.880
are written by scientists.

00:49:34.880 --> 00:49:38.809
They're written in collaboration with our
journalists so that they're accessible but

00:49:38.809 --> 00:49:40.530
they're written by scientists.

00:49:40.530 --> 00:49:41.819
About their work.

00:49:41.819 --> 00:49:47.020
In the past we used to let the scientists
write in kind of a lecture style as if everything

00:49:47.020 --> 00:49:51.711
was solved and they would look over the past
three or four years and it kind of came out

00:49:51.711 --> 00:49:53.230
like here's all the received wisdom.

00:49:53.230 --> 00:50:00.060
A thing that I tried to do and I think is
an example of I'd like to think, I hope to

00:50:00.060 --> 00:50:08.010
think a productive way to approach it is tell
us your story as if it's what I did last summer.

00:50:08.010 --> 00:50:09.200
What was your question?

00:50:09.200 --> 00:50:11.109
How did you try to explore it?

00:50:11.109 --> 00:50:13.109
When you made a mistake what happened there?

00:50:13.109 --> 00:50:14.230
How did you get past it?

00:50:14.230 --> 00:50:15.470
Who did you talk to you?

00:50:15.470 --> 00:50:19.990
And so I actually invited the researchers,
and increasingly we do this at Scientific

00:50:19.990 --> 00:50:25.490
American, to speak from a first person point
of view about them and their teams and embrace

00:50:25.490 --> 00:50:28.430
the fact that science is meandering.

00:50:28.430 --> 00:50:33.849
That we do make mistakes, that sometimes we
see things in the data that because of confirmation

00:50:33.849 --> 00:50:39.940
bias or a mistake in the reagent or a counterfeit,
you know, technology that we bought that we

00:50:39.940 --> 00:50:40.940
didn't know.

00:50:40.940 --> 00:50:44.369
These things happen because it is a product
of human endeavor.

00:50:44.369 --> 00:50:49.490
But because science is a process and an evidence
based one, eventually we right that over time.

00:50:49.490 --> 00:50:53.670
Meaning we right in the sense of r-i-g-h-t,
rather than write it out.

00:50:53.670 --> 00:50:56.710
So I think that's one way we can contribute.

00:50:56.710 --> 00:50:58.099
Take the longer view.

00:50:58.099 --> 00:51:02.589
Don't just be slavish to the thing that maybe
just happened that's the sharp headline but

00:51:02.589 --> 00:51:04.079
try to provide the context.

00:51:04.079 --> 00:51:08.690
And I think that's one place where at least
the journalism world that we participate in,

00:51:08.690 --> 00:51:10.769
we can benefit this process.

00:51:10.769 --> 00:51:16.109
I mean Carl you asked me about creating Retraction
Watch what made us want to do that.

00:51:16.109 --> 00:51:20.369
I think one of the answers that I could also
give is that we wanted to write about that

00:51:20.369 --> 00:51:21.809
process of science.

00:51:21.809 --> 00:51:26.509
You know I have to say and so I was at Reuters
Health, I was running Reuters Health at the

00:51:26.509 --> 00:51:27.509
time.

00:51:27.509 --> 00:51:29.839
Reuters I'm sure you all know is a large wire
service.

00:51:29.839 --> 00:51:34.380
And Reuters survives, like most wire services
do, on volume.

00:51:34.380 --> 00:51:38.829
And our clients were very clear about how
much volume they wanted and how do you write,

00:51:38.829 --> 00:51:42.670
and I can give you the numbers but it's somewhat
staggering and I don't want to scare people

00:51:42.670 --> 00:51:47.539
but, how do you create that many stories per
week doing something other than writing about

00:51:47.539 --> 00:51:49.160
every study that comes out that?

00:51:49.160 --> 00:51:53.400
I'd like to think that we in some ways we're
for a professional audience, medical audience

00:51:53.400 --> 00:51:56.950
that understood that, but and I like to think
we put the kind of context in that you would

00:51:56.950 --> 00:52:01.770
see like in Health News Review which is a
great site that tells you, gives ideas about

00:52:01.770 --> 00:52:04.340
how to cover medical studies in particular.

00:52:04.340 --> 00:52:08.920
You know the sort of the the triad of coffee
chocolate and red wine.

00:52:08.920 --> 00:52:09.920
Right?

00:52:09.920 --> 00:52:13.440
I think that if no reporter ever wrote a story
about chocolate, coffee or red wine again

00:52:13.440 --> 00:52:14.849
we'd all be better off.

00:52:14.849 --> 00:52:16.760
I like two of those things.

00:52:16.760 --> 00:52:18.220
The other one I don't actually drink.

00:52:18.220 --> 00:52:20.220
That's OK.

00:52:20.220 --> 00:52:23.690
But the point is I actually I wouldn't say
I had whiplash but I had a little cognitive

00:52:23.690 --> 00:52:28.589
dissonance because here I was in my day job
running a website that you know not a website

00:52:28.589 --> 00:52:33.020
but a wire service that had to pump out the
volume because we were reporting on single

00:52:33.020 --> 00:52:35.319
studies, dozens of them every day.

00:52:35.319 --> 00:52:40.759
And yet I was sort of thinking trying to think
a little bit more globally and slowing things

00:52:40.759 --> 00:52:44.290
down and thinking about the experience of
science and what it all meant.

00:52:44.290 --> 00:52:45.640
Maybe that was useful actually.

00:52:45.640 --> 00:52:48.289
Maybe it sort of connected some dots for me.

00:52:48.289 --> 00:52:52.799
But I think that's I think it would be great
if more journalists did the sort of work that

00:52:52.799 --> 00:52:58.410
Mariette was describing which is in which
I think this salon is very much about which

00:52:58.410 --> 00:53:01.480
is about how science really works.

00:53:01.480 --> 00:53:07.020
We all need to get excited about what science
is doing for us but how do you really include

00:53:07.020 --> 00:53:08.170
at least some of that.

00:53:08.170 --> 00:53:13.480
Well I just want to touch on that before I
let the scientists pass judgment on the journalists.

00:53:13.480 --> 00:53:21.650
The thing is that like I think when I became
a fan of Retraction Watch and then I started

00:53:21.650 --> 00:53:25.509
reading about some of these people that were
featured there, which you know I started to

00:53:25.509 --> 00:53:30.930
see more and more of these longer pieces about
some of the more notorious characters.

00:53:30.930 --> 00:53:33.079
I you you you.

00:53:33.079 --> 00:53:37.450
It was hard to look away because in the most
extreme cases where people would have to have

00:53:37.450 --> 00:53:41.740
like twenty, thirty what's the record for
one person having to retract?

00:53:41.740 --> 00:53:42.740
183 actually.

00:53:42.740 --> 00:53:46.480
183 papers from one scientist that had to
be retracted and you start reading about these

00:53:46.480 --> 00:53:52.240
people and they're just like they're sociopaths
and fascinating and you're like how did this

00:53:52.240 --> 00:53:55.240
person go years and years and years lying
to everyone around them?

00:53:55.240 --> 00:53:57.900
I mean that's... we love reading about that.

00:53:57.900 --> 00:54:07.470
Is there a risk there that if you, that we're
going to swap this sort of naive idea of science,

00:54:07.470 --> 00:54:09.730
scientist as hero who is only about the truth.

00:54:09.730 --> 00:54:14.119
Are we gonna swap that for you know scientist
as sociopath trying to get the next grant?

00:54:14.119 --> 00:54:15.430
I mean is that a risk?

00:54:15.430 --> 00:54:16.930
Absolutely it is.

00:54:16.930 --> 00:54:19.190
And it's something that you know I wouldn't
say we thought of on day one but that hit

00:54:19.190 --> 00:54:22.740
us sort of smacked us in the face quite early.

00:54:22.740 --> 00:54:26.930
In the way that we try and combat that if
you will both in our own minds and our own

00:54:26.930 --> 00:54:28.329
work but also in terms of the public.

00:54:28.329 --> 00:54:32.990
So one thing that has happened I don't know
that it would surprise anyone to learn that

00:54:32.990 --> 00:54:38.920
a lot of what I would sort of frankly call
anti-science forces advocates have seized

00:54:38.920 --> 00:54:39.920
upon what we do.

00:54:39.920 --> 00:54:41.230
We're very popular with them.

00:54:41.230 --> 00:54:45.390
Not all of them, but with some of them and
so we're very conscious of that.

00:54:45.390 --> 00:54:48.960
One of the things that we've done to try and
combat that is to create something called,

00:54:48.960 --> 00:54:52.710
this gets back to something that Mariette
was talking about in terms of trying to correct

00:54:52.710 --> 00:54:56.989
the record, doing the right thing, We have
a "doing the right thing" award that we created

00:54:56.989 --> 00:55:01.079
actually with Stat so it's me and Adam and
our column there at Stat.

00:55:01.079 --> 00:55:03.900
We handed out the first one just about a month
ago.

00:55:03.900 --> 00:55:08.220
And the idea there is to reward people who
do the right thing by correcting the record

00:55:08.220 --> 00:55:11.730
at some costs to themselves because, you know
correcting the record is great.

00:55:11.730 --> 00:55:12.730
Very important.

00:55:12.730 --> 00:55:14.859
But you actually may face consequences if
you do that.

00:55:14.859 --> 00:55:19.359
And I think we talked about what some of those
consequences can be and we call it, it's "doing

00:55:19.359 --> 00:55:23.609
the right thing" so it's called the "Dirt"
award which is almost the acronym for it and

00:55:23.609 --> 00:55:26.510
sometimes you have to get dirty to clean up
the center.

00:55:26.510 --> 00:55:28.450
We like to market stuff too.

00:55:28.450 --> 00:55:32.299
I think that if you if you approach it and
tell those stories as well, really try to

00:55:32.299 --> 00:55:37.540
tell those stories as well I think you at
least over time can tell the whole picture.

00:55:37.540 --> 00:55:38.540
You know.

00:55:38.540 --> 00:55:42.089
So before we turn to some questions I just
want to hear from the scientists about what

00:55:42.089 --> 00:55:50.059
you how you feel sort of the journalism world
fits into the issues that we've been raising

00:55:50.059 --> 00:55:53.000
about you know the competition, the pressures
and so on.

00:55:53.000 --> 00:56:01.880
You know I mean is the way that the journalism
is covering it kind of making things worse

00:56:01.880 --> 00:56:02.880
or better.

00:56:02.880 --> 00:56:05.109
I mean how what's your perspective on it?

00:56:05.109 --> 00:56:09.869
The preferred answer of the scientist is I
never said that, the journalist made it up.

00:56:09.869 --> 00:56:16.930
And sometimes that does happen or at least
there is some some major difference between

00:56:16.930 --> 00:56:18.810
what comes out of my mouth and what comes
into print.

00:56:18.810 --> 00:56:24.369
More often what happens is the scientist would
say I know I said that but I prefer that I

00:56:24.369 --> 00:56:25.369
didn't.

00:56:25.369 --> 00:56:26.369
You know.

00:56:26.369 --> 00:56:31.369
We we're always getting these you know people
demanding to see our articles in advance.

00:56:31.369 --> 00:56:36.980
I just want to check my quotes cause maybe
I said something I shouldn't have.

00:56:36.980 --> 00:56:39.650
I think that one of the things that is changing
the equation.

00:56:39.650 --> 00:56:44.509
Well two of the things that are changing the
equation is one, as you know, journalism is

00:56:44.509 --> 00:56:51.819
now in its own interesting situation and I
have friends who are journalists and it's

00:56:51.819 --> 00:56:57.490
not easy to find, maintain a job as a journalist
or often you're a freelancer.

00:56:57.490 --> 00:57:01.859
That means even more pressure, even less time
to check stories even more pressure to go

00:57:01.859 --> 00:57:04.640
for the sexy stuff and all that.

00:57:04.640 --> 00:57:05.850
So that's one side of the equation.

00:57:05.850 --> 00:57:11.099
The other side is more and more young scientists
especially beginning with PhD students and

00:57:11.099 --> 00:57:14.730
post-docs and young investigators on tenure
track.

00:57:14.730 --> 00:57:16.430
They start actually writing their own stuff.

00:57:16.430 --> 00:57:17.480
They write blogs.

00:57:17.480 --> 00:57:19.569
You know I write two blogs.

00:57:19.569 --> 00:57:21.910
So I communicate directly to the public.

00:57:21.910 --> 00:57:27.599
That that's interesting because then I have
journalists who come to me and want to talk

00:57:27.599 --> 00:57:30.529
about something that I publish on my blog
not something to publish to the department

00:57:30.529 --> 00:57:31.739
of literature.

00:57:31.739 --> 00:57:35.230
And so that I think adds an interesting…I
mean I don't know where that it's going.

00:57:35.230 --> 00:57:40.660
At the moment we're kind of welcoming it because
I do think that there is a good thing to add

00:57:40.660 --> 00:57:42.750
the voices directly of the scientist.

00:57:42.750 --> 00:57:45.310
Scientists should be more conscious about
talking to the general public.

00:57:45.310 --> 00:57:49.800
After all as you were saying not only our
education but most of our money for research

00:57:49.800 --> 00:57:53.630
and so forth does come directly or indirectly
from taxpayers.

00:57:53.630 --> 00:57:58.220
And so it's just right that we spend some
of the time talking back to those people and

00:57:58.220 --> 00:58:01.150
say, hey, you know here's what I'm doing and
why it is interesting.

00:58:01.150 --> 00:58:02.529
So that's part of being a scientist.

00:58:02.529 --> 00:58:04.130
That is part I think of being scientists.

00:58:04.130 --> 00:58:07.970
And now it has been of course historically
this was a problem because your colleagues

00:58:07.970 --> 00:58:10.549
will look at you and say you're wasting your
time.

00:58:10.549 --> 00:58:13.670
Either you're wasting your time or you're
not a good enough scientist and that's why

00:58:13.670 --> 00:58:16.290
you're doing that instead of another grant
proposal.

00:58:16.290 --> 00:58:17.700
I think that's changing.

00:58:17.700 --> 00:58:24.569
You know Planck famously said…Max Planck,
famously said that ideas in science get accepted

00:58:24.569 --> 00:58:27.130
one funeral at a time you know because the
old people die.

00:58:27.130 --> 00:58:32.960
I think that's true also for other attitudes
within science that is the old guard is retiring

00:58:32.960 --> 00:58:34.999
and getting out of the way.

00:58:34.999 --> 00:58:41.180
And so the new people have grown up with social
networks and Twitter and blogs and things

00:58:41.180 --> 00:58:44.760
like that and I think that actually is almost
an unqualified good at the moment.

00:58:44.760 --> 00:58:47.450
We'll see how that evolves for the next decade.

00:58:47.450 --> 00:58:50.200
Dany I'll give you the last word before we
start taking questions…

00:58:50.200 --> 00:58:51.200
I agree completely.

00:58:51.200 --> 00:58:56.749
I think that so I don't begin to understand
the constraints that a journalist faces.

00:58:56.749 --> 00:59:01.499
You know I have two inches to describe something
that took someone six years.

00:59:01.499 --> 00:59:08.089
And when you have to go from the boring details
that make it science and make it trustworthy

00:59:08.089 --> 00:59:11.650
and pick out whoo, this is the wow right?

00:59:11.650 --> 00:59:14.130
We call it the gee whiz stuff.

00:59:14.130 --> 00:59:21.099
You lose something and it can come out wrong
and I know that when I've worked with journalists

00:59:21.099 --> 00:59:27.319
I asked to see it, to check the facts not
to check that I come off a certain way or.

00:59:27.319 --> 00:59:31.800
But but facts can change when you change the
structure of the sentence.

00:59:31.800 --> 00:59:38.170
If I if it takes me twenty words to say something
because I'm trying to be really accurate and

00:59:38.170 --> 00:59:43.559
really precise and not let it get blown out
of proportion.

00:59:43.559 --> 00:59:49.160
And you have to trim it down to six then it
becomes a sentence that you know you used

00:59:49.160 --> 00:59:50.319
the word proof.

00:59:50.319 --> 00:59:55.050
I used the word I'll say well I have evidence
that it's not this or that you know it takes

00:59:55.050 --> 00:59:56.050
me...

00:59:56.050 --> 00:59:57.109
Yeah, yeah, yeah, but you proved it…

00:59:57.109 --> 00:59:59.040
Right and then that becomes proved.

00:59:59.040 --> 01:00:06.349
And now - You have a headline to write and
now people think that scientists prove things.

01:00:06.349 --> 01:00:09.660
And that I think is is is difficult.

01:00:09.660 --> 01:00:16.799
I don't know what the answer is except a very
deep cultural shift and I hope things like

01:00:16.799 --> 01:00:19.470
this festival will encourage it.

01:00:19.470 --> 01:00:24.579
But there was a time where you needed to know
science to be considered an educated person

01:00:24.579 --> 01:00:26.980
and now you need to know the humanities you
know.

01:00:26.980 --> 01:00:31.730
You know I don't like Shakespeare you know.

01:00:31.730 --> 01:00:39.609
But I think that you shouldn't be able to
avoid learning biology because you're an English

01:00:39.609 --> 01:00:40.609
major.

01:00:40.609 --> 01:00:47.230
I think that we have to go back to a time
where even if you do use the word prove, people

01:00:47.230 --> 01:00:50.230
understand what that means in the context
of science.

01:00:50.230 --> 01:00:56.309
They, they can read that and say oh OK what
that means, you know it's just it's automatic

01:00:56.309 --> 01:01:03.230
that they will interpret that as we, we have
a lot of evidence that supports this idea

01:01:03.230 --> 01:01:05.809
so we're running with it.

01:01:05.809 --> 01:01:12.309
And if, and if that was part of our citizenship
almost I think that would that would help

01:01:12.309 --> 01:01:13.329
a great deal.

01:01:13.329 --> 01:01:15.480
I don't know how to make it happen.

01:01:15.480 --> 01:01:16.480
OK.

01:01:16.480 --> 01:01:17.480
So can we.

01:01:17.480 --> 01:01:23.319
Well it's a little hard for me to see but
could we field some questions?

01:01:23.319 --> 01:01:25.619
Oh there is an audience!

01:01:25.619 --> 01:01:27.940
Hey look at that!

01:01:27.940 --> 01:01:32.170
So we do have a microphone to pass out.

01:01:32.170 --> 01:01:33.999
All right, so raise your hands high.

01:01:33.999 --> 01:01:36.579
How about we start with that person.

01:01:36.579 --> 01:01:37.579
OK.

01:01:37.579 --> 01:01:42.650
How much of this problem in communicating
about science might be due to the fact that

01:01:42.650 --> 01:01:51.460
you have some people, some philosophers who
would argue that scientific truth is as relative

01:01:51.460 --> 01:01:55.240
a truth as it is simply as good as any other
form of truth.

01:01:55.240 --> 01:01:58.210
I'm talking about the relativists and deconstructionists.

01:01:58.210 --> 01:02:01.569
I'd be interested to hear what the panel would
say.

01:02:01.569 --> 01:02:07.640
Well since I'm the resident philosopher on
the panel I'll take that one right.

01:02:07.640 --> 01:02:08.640
Yeah.

01:02:08.640 --> 01:02:12.789
So there is an issue with that but I really
believe I'm not I'm not usually an optimist

01:02:12.789 --> 01:02:16.650
but I believe that that's done and over with
pretty much.

01:02:16.650 --> 01:02:20.750
This was these so-called science wars mostly
that happened in the 1990s.

01:02:20.750 --> 01:02:28.130
There was a small but vocal set of postmodernist
philosophers and sociologists, it wasn't just

01:02:28.130 --> 01:02:32.809
philosophers who started going around saying,
well ‘science is socially constructed’

01:02:32.809 --> 01:02:34.200
which of course is true.

01:02:34.200 --> 01:02:37.259
We were saying earlier you know several times
science is a social enterprise.

01:02:37.259 --> 01:02:39.830
It is socially constructed, in a sense in
a sense.

01:02:39.830 --> 01:02:43.660
In another sense of course that social construction
is not independent of the outside world.

01:02:43.660 --> 01:02:47.109
It's very highly constrained by the outside
world.

01:02:47.109 --> 01:02:53.340
The response to that sort of extreme version
of postmodernism actually came both from scientists,

01:02:53.340 --> 01:02:58.640
Alan Sokal here at NYU for instance, and especially
by philosophers of science.

01:02:58.640 --> 01:03:04.099
Philosophers of science were very strong in
responding to that to that sort of extreme

01:03:04.099 --> 01:03:07.829
criticism of science and I think actually
we are in a good place at the moment between

01:03:07.829 --> 01:03:12.619
the two disciplines because there is a good
balance where the scientists themselves tend

01:03:12.619 --> 01:03:17.910
to realize that, yes that there is in fact
quite a bit more of the human side to doing

01:03:17.910 --> 01:03:18.910
science.

01:03:18.910 --> 01:03:19.910
There is mistakes, there is fraud.

01:03:19.910 --> 01:03:20.910
There is all sorts of stuff.

01:03:20.910 --> 01:03:21.910
All right.

01:03:21.910 --> 01:03:25.519
Do you have the microphone for someone else?

01:03:25.519 --> 01:03:32.430
So my question is a topic which has been briefly
touched upon which is replicating research

01:03:32.430 --> 01:03:35.549
and that there are no real incentives for
that.

01:03:35.549 --> 01:03:37.009
Have any of you.

01:03:37.009 --> 01:03:45.890
Do any of you have any ideas about how there
might be incentives to replicate studies?

01:03:45.890 --> 01:03:51.660
Perhaps work into the system like a lot of
professors at universities are required to

01:03:51.660 --> 01:03:53.079
teach.

01:03:53.079 --> 01:04:01.009
Maybe graduate students are required to replicate
research before moving on to original stuff?

01:04:01.009 --> 01:04:02.009
Yeah.

01:04:02.009 --> 01:04:04.079
I'll address that.

01:04:04.079 --> 01:04:08.640
I want to qualify that there's no incentive
to replicate.

01:04:08.640 --> 01:04:09.799
Often.

01:04:09.799 --> 01:04:12.479
So all results are comparisons.

01:04:12.479 --> 01:04:14.619
You're always comparing what you've got to
something else.

01:04:14.619 --> 01:04:18.400
And in many cases what you're comparing to
is what the last guy got.

01:04:18.400 --> 01:04:21.930
So you're replicating it as one of your controls.

01:04:21.930 --> 01:04:25.789
So it does happen.

01:04:25.789 --> 01:04:27.489
What you suggest is a great idea.

01:04:27.489 --> 01:04:35.619
And one way of teaching and getting your students
kind of up to whatever boundary you're pushing

01:04:35.619 --> 01:04:37.789
is to have them replicate what's been done.

01:04:37.789 --> 01:04:40.079
That's how you can learn a technique.

01:04:40.079 --> 01:04:47.849
That's how you can really understand what
what the person, you're your competitor found.

01:04:47.849 --> 01:04:48.930
Do you agree with it?

01:04:48.930 --> 01:04:50.650
Don't you agree with it?

01:04:50.650 --> 01:04:52.609
And you can use that as a teaching tool.

01:04:52.609 --> 01:04:56.640
So there's ways of sneaking it in.

01:04:56.640 --> 01:05:01.269
You can't just publish a paper where you replicate
what everybody else did.

01:05:01.269 --> 01:05:08.960
But often you can include the replication
as that which you are building upon.

01:05:08.960 --> 01:05:10.349
So here's where we started.

01:05:10.349 --> 01:05:11.869
Yes we agree.

01:05:11.869 --> 01:05:13.130
But now look at this.

01:05:13.130 --> 01:05:17.029
And so I think that it's.

01:05:17.029 --> 01:05:19.920
It's not as clear cut as we we have no incentive
to replicate.

01:05:19.920 --> 01:05:22.170
I think it's you can use it.

01:05:22.170 --> 01:05:24.579
Any other ideas about fixing the replication.

01:05:24.579 --> 01:05:32.299
Well I think too, if I can consider two quick
opposite sides of the same coin maybe.

01:05:32.299 --> 01:05:38.390
One is that I would argue and it gets back
to something Massimo was saying earlier that

01:05:38.390 --> 01:05:42.759
I would actually argue that some people have
made their careers or are starting to in this

01:05:42.759 --> 01:05:44.460
case, I think things are changing.

01:05:44.460 --> 01:05:46.930
Not so much in replicating but actually failing
to replicate.

01:05:46.930 --> 01:05:51.260
Now that creates its own sort of bias because
if you become known as the person who always

01:05:51.260 --> 01:05:56.059
goes out and fails to replicate then you kind
of show up to the saloon with a you know a

01:05:56.059 --> 01:05:58.950
couple of guns or something, six shooters.

01:05:58.950 --> 01:06:03.289
But so that's but that there can be a sort
of incentive of its own to do that.

01:06:03.289 --> 01:06:07.569
And the other is probably talking out of the
other side of my mouth when I was talking

01:06:07.569 --> 01:06:09.220
earlier about we have too many metrics.

01:06:09.220 --> 01:06:12.680
Well if we're going to be stuck with metrics
well we could make this into a metric you

01:06:12.680 --> 01:06:16.820
could have something out of and I reckon you
know sort of at a very high level called for

01:06:16.820 --> 01:06:22.339
a bunch years ago, a transparency index like
you know out of a given journal, out of a

01:06:22.339 --> 01:06:25.329
given maybe lab, out of a given sort of field.

01:06:25.329 --> 01:06:28.400
What are the, what is the likelihood that
that's that something actually holds up and

01:06:28.400 --> 01:06:33.279
if you turn that into a positive if you say
well the higher it is the better then maybe

01:06:33.279 --> 01:06:34.589
you'll have something that sticks.

01:06:34.589 --> 01:06:37.960
But again those are not particularly well-formed
ideas to be honest

01:06:37.960 --> 01:06:44.839
I mean what about I mean on the journalism
side I mean what could we do to sort of let

01:06:44.839 --> 01:06:56.220
people know that that one little crazy sounding
paper in Science we'll say is you know it

01:06:56.220 --> 01:06:57.490
may not be the last word.

01:06:57.490 --> 01:07:00.619
You know it turned out that was wrong.

01:07:00.619 --> 01:07:06.019
The article you wrote no or I mean how do
we sort of build in that process of replication

01:07:06.019 --> 01:07:08.400
into the journalism about science?

01:07:08.400 --> 01:07:13.130
I mean, maybe I'm gonna sound like a broken
record at this point because part of what

01:07:13.130 --> 01:07:18.490
we do when we choose what we cover is we celebrate
things in effect because we we've chosen that

01:07:18.490 --> 01:07:21.849
we've curated a story and presented it.

01:07:21.849 --> 01:07:26.210
I think one way we do that is we portray the
lives of scientists.

01:07:26.210 --> 01:07:28.829
We portray the what is it like a lab?

01:07:28.829 --> 01:07:34.229
The Times used to have a piece that I loved
a lot which was about working life of scientists.

01:07:34.229 --> 01:07:38.099
I've had many researchers tell me that you
know you have no idea how long I spent writing

01:07:38.099 --> 01:07:42.240
this software program so I could do this piece
of research on cosmology.

01:07:42.240 --> 01:07:46.230
It's all of that that we should celebrate
as well not just the result that pops out.

01:07:46.230 --> 01:07:50.319
So yah, that's one way we can contribute.

01:07:50.319 --> 01:07:55.390
We were talking a little about the discussion
section in experiments and how that's really

01:07:55.390 --> 01:07:59.170
just not the science of it and yet it's the
most interesting part.

01:07:59.170 --> 01:08:04.339
So I'm a philosophy student and a lot of what
we do when I'm in classes doing research is

01:08:04.339 --> 01:08:08.979
we look at the discussion section in science
pieces and so I'm not really reading the rest

01:08:08.979 --> 01:08:10.869
of the experiment or I am but like briefly.

01:08:10.869 --> 01:08:14.660
I'm looking at what their conclusion was and
what they're discussing.

01:08:14.660 --> 01:08:19.490
So really should we be taking a lot out of
that and like writing our own papers off of

01:08:19.490 --> 01:08:22.339
it or should we be used to looking at the
experiment side?

01:08:22.339 --> 01:08:23.339
Wow.

01:08:23.339 --> 01:08:26.170
That's a great question.

01:08:26.170 --> 01:08:31.920
The discussion section is part of the science.

01:08:31.920 --> 01:08:37.029
It's the most educated mind at that point,
the person who did the work and got those

01:08:37.029 --> 01:08:38.389
results.

01:08:38.389 --> 01:08:44.230
At that moment that is the most educated guess
and it's often right.

01:08:44.230 --> 01:08:46.159
You develop experience.

01:08:46.159 --> 01:08:49.359
You develop, you know, you learn as you go.

01:08:49.359 --> 01:08:53.200
So it's certainly part of the science and
the interpretation is very interesting and

01:08:53.200 --> 01:08:56.109
putting it in context is very interesting.

01:08:56.109 --> 01:09:04.259
But I would say that you might be able to
put a layer of depth into your understanding

01:09:04.259 --> 01:09:09.920
of how we got to that if you look at the results,
if you look at the part that is just here

01:09:09.920 --> 01:09:12.119
is what we've got.

01:09:12.119 --> 01:09:17.120
And I, and then say you know the process that
we go through.

01:09:17.120 --> 01:09:18.120
OK.

01:09:18.120 --> 01:09:20.859
How many different ways could I have gotten
the same thing?

01:09:20.859 --> 01:09:24.299
And then you go through and then you go in
the discussion and you say well I might have

01:09:24.299 --> 01:09:29.400
gotten it if A was true but I could also gotten
it if B was true.

01:09:29.400 --> 01:09:35.989
And that process of forcing yourself to not
fall in love with one interpretation.

01:09:35.989 --> 01:09:40.970
The danger of falling in love with an idea.

01:09:40.970 --> 01:09:41.970
And we do.

01:09:41.970 --> 01:09:44.489
We fall in love with our ideas.

01:09:44.489 --> 01:09:45.920
It's very creative.

01:09:45.920 --> 01:09:51.230
And I think that if you were to go and look
at the difference between what's in the results

01:09:51.230 --> 01:09:55.170
section, where they say this is what happened.

01:09:55.170 --> 01:10:01.650
And how that gets turned into a discussion
section where there's the philosophical underpinnings

01:10:01.650 --> 01:10:04.840
of it where you're thinking about necessity
and sufficiency.

01:10:04.840 --> 01:10:12.369
And you're thinking about all those those
very basic intellectual processes that are

01:10:12.369 --> 01:10:13.369
behind it.

01:10:13.369 --> 01:10:18.900
I think that you might get something different
and very interesting comparing the you know

01:10:18.900 --> 01:10:23.770
just the facts ma'am to here's how I interpret
this and here's what I think is important

01:10:23.770 --> 01:10:24.770
about it.

01:10:24.770 --> 01:10:26.610
I think that would be an interesting way to
look at it.

01:10:26.610 --> 01:10:27.909
But there's an additional problem there, right.

01:10:27.909 --> 01:10:31.940
So there is a method problem if you will,
which is scientific papers are in a sense

01:10:31.940 --> 01:10:35.380
a piece of fiction.

01:10:35.380 --> 01:10:38.239
Some of them actually are fiction.

01:10:38.239 --> 01:10:43.050
Some of them really are fiction but all of
them to some extent are if fact a piece of

01:10:43.050 --> 01:10:47.510
fiction in the sense that it really didn't
happen like that.

01:10:47.510 --> 01:10:50.570
This is the structure of the paper is incredibly
artificial.

01:10:50.570 --> 01:10:51.570
There's this introduction…

01:10:51.570 --> 01:10:56.070
You’re saying like, I did this and then
I did this… and then I had this great idea…

01:10:56.070 --> 01:10:57.900
…First of all…You don't even do that.

01:10:57.900 --> 01:11:00.199
You write in third person.

01:11:00.199 --> 01:11:01.580
They did that.

01:11:01.580 --> 01:11:02.960
We did that.

01:11:02.960 --> 01:11:05.420
Right, passive voice.

01:11:05.420 --> 01:11:07.010
That's changing fortunately.

01:11:07.010 --> 01:11:10.510
So it's it's like you're supposed to be distancing
yourself as much as possible.

01:11:10.510 --> 01:11:11.510
Right.

01:11:11.510 --> 01:11:12.610
So this is a very structured thing.

01:11:12.610 --> 01:11:17.130
The introduction, what tools and methods,
the results and the conclusion and the discussion.

01:11:17.130 --> 01:11:22.889
And they're supposed to have these really
different functions as if you're sort of pretending

01:11:22.889 --> 01:11:25.639
that when you write the introduction that
you really don't know at that point how things

01:11:25.639 --> 01:11:26.679
are gonna turn out.

01:11:26.679 --> 01:11:30.210
But of course you've written the introduction
after you've done experiments after you've

01:11:30.210 --> 01:11:31.429
done the data analysis.

01:11:31.429 --> 01:11:34.820
In fact typically the way I used I don't know
how other people do it but the way I taught

01:11:34.820 --> 01:11:38.090
my students to writing the papers was never
to start with the introduction.

01:11:38.090 --> 01:11:39.800
You always start with the results.

01:11:39.800 --> 01:11:43.900
Because you want to have the results in good
shape and then you know the diagrams and tables

01:11:43.900 --> 01:11:44.900
and all that.

01:11:44.900 --> 01:11:49.590
After you've done that then you sort of write,
you write the actual narrative that goes with

01:11:49.590 --> 01:11:50.590
the results.

01:11:50.590 --> 01:11:53.960
Then maybe you're going back to a little bit
of introduction, discussion and you go back

01:11:53.960 --> 01:11:55.640
and forth until everything fits together.

01:11:55.640 --> 01:11:57.050
So it's a work of fiction.

01:11:57.050 --> 01:12:00.770
It's everything is put in a way that it's
not the way it actually went, but…I don't

01:12:00.770 --> 01:12:05.800
mean fiction in the ways that you obviously
have that problematic but it does give the

01:12:05.800 --> 01:12:10.460
impression, even to the scientists themselves,
that there is a lot more objectivity, a lot

01:12:10.460 --> 01:12:15.010
more rigor a lot more sort of well it had
to be that way than there really is.

01:12:15.010 --> 01:12:17.840
Science is much more fun than that, but it's
much more unpredictable.

01:12:17.840 --> 01:12:23.300
Imagine if you were actually running a scientific
paper the way in which you write a novel or

01:12:23.300 --> 01:12:26.739
or little you know short stories like oh we
started out this way.

01:12:26.739 --> 01:12:30.390
We were having coffee that morning when my
graduate student he said you know ‘damn,

01:12:30.390 --> 01:12:31.520
we don’t understand this.’

01:12:31.520 --> 01:12:35.500
That would be much more true to the way things
actually happened.

01:12:35.500 --> 01:12:36.989
I'm not suggesting that's practical.

01:12:36.989 --> 01:12:40.520
But you know what you need to keep in mind
that when you read a scientific paper you

01:12:40.520 --> 01:12:43.080
there is a lot of rearranging of stuff going
on.

01:12:43.080 --> 01:12:47.739
When I interview somebody for an article I'm
always just, I just kind of like set the paper

01:12:47.739 --> 01:12:48.760
aside and say hey come on how did this really
happen?

01:12:48.760 --> 01:12:54.369
And you get a bizarre story that you never
would have guessed from reading the paper.

01:12:54.369 --> 01:12:55.369
Much more interesting.

01:12:55.369 --> 01:12:56.369
Yes.

01:12:56.369 --> 01:12:58.160
I’d just like to thank everybody for attending
today.

01:12:58.160 --> 01:12:59.320
So thank you all for coming.

